+# Portfolio bundled Clerk UI verification — 24 October 2026
+
+> Packaging note (25 October 2026): This report retains its dated local verification and prior-work attribution. Raw logs, screenshots, traces and machine receipts remain locally retained, not newly published CI evidence. The [sanitised summary](evidence/portfolio-reviewed-source-2026-10-25.md) records the carried hashes, source equivalence, current canonical health and packaging checks. This isolated branch has not rerun the application tests or build.
+
+The supported bundled-UI path fixes the ten decorative social-icon HTML errors while keeping Clerk's official sign-in/sign-up implementation. This is a local source repair; production delivery still requires the existing CI-gated release path and its account prerequisites.
+
+The [sanitised Markdown evidence summary](evidence/portfolio-reviewed-source-2026-10-25.md) records the tested source digest and retained machine-report hashes. The original source/build/browser receipts remain local, outside this review branch. The [prior report](portfolio-task-verification-2026-10-24.md) and all of its artifacts are preserved as the failing HTML baseline, with source digest `a85daffc19adc44264c32a90292c5e7bcd84d6790ba2f6697eded025acd6cf01`.
+
+## Supported integration and narrow patch
+
+Clerk documents `import { ui } from "@clerk/ui"` with `<ClerkProvider ui={ui}>` for [Next.js component versioning](https://clerk.com/docs/reference/components/versioning). Installed Next SDK 7.9.20 inherits the public `ui` prop from React 6.18.1. Its loader uses the supplied `ClerkUI` constructor instead of fetching the UI constructor from the CDN.
+
+The project now pins `@clerk/ui` to exactly `1.41.2`. Both existing providers in `src/components/site/auth/clerk-auth-widgets.tsx` receive `ui={ui}` and the documented `prefetchUI={false}` option. Providers remain scoped to the two authentication routes; no global provider was added. The explicit prefetch option is needed because this SDK’s App Router script renderer emits a CDN UI preload without checking the supplied `ui` object. The first diagnostic observed four unnecessary preload requests across the two forms; the final probe verifies their removal. This does not disable the locally supplied constructor. See the [provider options](https://clerk.com/docs/nextjs/reference/components/clerk-provider).
+
+The published MIT package includes readable `dist/common/ProviderIcon.js`, which `SocialButtons.js` imports directly. `patches/@clerk__ui@1.41.2.patch` makes one property conditional:
+
+```diff
+- "aria-label": alt || `${name} icon`,
++ "aria-label": rest["aria-hidden"] === true || rest["aria-hidden"] === "true" ? undefined : alt || `${name} icon`,
+```
+
+Only icons explicitly hidden from assistive technology lose the redundant label. Other icon labels, visible images, button names, authentication requests and the complete official flow logic remain unchanged. The component renders correct markup directly; no runtime DOM rewrite or validation filter is used. The exact-version patch is registered through pnpm's `patchedDependencies` and bound into the lockfile. The existing strict HTML failure provides the negative baseline; the unchanged browser validator supplies the positive result.
+
+## Loading behavior and maintenance
+
+The public npm index is 452 bytes, but that is not the total runtime cost: it imports the constructor and bootstrap. The large SignIn/SignUp components and supporting providers use dynamic imports and `React.lazy`. The real network probe records the observed loading behavior: the anonymous homepage has no Clerk global, remote Clerk requests or bundled UI/widget bootstrap, and both authentication pages load local UI/widget chunks with no remote UI JavaScript requests. Social buttons retain their accessible names. The evidence reports decoded response-body bytes, not compressed transfer sizes or a comparative speed claim.
+
+The package archive is approximately 3.04 MB and its unpacked metadata is approximately 17.64 MB, including alternate/CDN builds and maps; those figures do not represent bytes fetched by a visitor. Bundling moves UI delivery onto the application's built assets. Core ClerkJS, backend authentication and provider resources remain external; this does not make authentication self-contained.
+
+UI fixes now arrive through deliberate dependency updates and deployment rather than automatic CDN UI updates. Review Clerk's security and UI changelogs when updating the pin, rebase or remove this patch as appropriate, and rerun strict HTML plus public/admin authentication checks. pnpm 11 throws on patch application failure, and unused patches fail by default. This uses the documented UI injection API with a locally maintained package patch; the patch itself is not an upstream Clerk release. See [Clerk pinning](https://clerk.com/docs/pinning) and [pnpm patch maintenance](https://pnpm.io/cli/patch).
+
+## Verification and original tasks
+
+The final source passes 370 unit tests with no skips and 100% reported line, branch and function coverage; build, lint/types, formatting, dead-code and the configured production audit gate pass. Public functional browser tests pass 190 cases with 22 declared skips, both admin cases pass, and the strict HTML case passes across 204 routes and 408 server/hydrated documents. The complete orchestrator exits 0. The real network probe confirms both named-button sets, local widget chunks and zero remote UI JavaScript requests.
+
+Nu reports **zero errors**, 123 warning-class informational messages (111 framework-generated script charset attributes, eight article-heading suggestions and four Clerk redundant aria-required attributes), and 8,306 other informational messages about React’s trailing slashes on void elements. The existing test’s severity treatment remains unchanged: it rejects every message whose type is not info. No warning/error filter was added or loosened. Full output and message counts are retained; this is not a claim of warning-free markup.
+
+The machine record contains terminal receipts, stable before/after source digests including the maintained patch, versions, sanitized logs, strict HTML output and real network/accessibility proof. It records current results rather than reusing earlier functional tests for the new source.
+
+The original Portfolio register remains:
+
+| Task                                      | Status           | Remaining acceptance                                                       |
+| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `review-portfolio-admin-email-refresh`    | Completed        | Retain current verified identity policy and passing regressions.           |
+| `review-portfolio-stage-health-promotion` | Locally verified | Actual CI stage, protected readiness, promotion and canonical attestation. |
+| `Grindhead/Portfolio:preview-environment` | Owner deferred   | Keep the owner's existing Preview deferral.                                |
+
+The last observed canonical deployment is `dpl_B5ca2VsdMDTn4aJKWgTL51mpmdtz`, revision `130686f829331bfc603ba128582cdac1361984aa`. An account-level CI startup gate and the protected CI credential gate remain; no candidate deployment or production promotion has occurred. The owner-authorized canonical protected-health check returned HTTP 200 with database and storage OK at `2026-10-24T04:44:25.543Z`, revision `130686f829331bfc603ba128582cdac1361984aa`. This verifies the existing canonical deployment only; the new candidate is not deployed, and its hosted CI, staged readiness and promotion acceptance remain pending.
+
+The new E2E run uses the repaired local-only media environment. Its disposable database and fixtures are cleaned, and Docker's original inactive state is restored only after verifying no other running containers. This run creates no new remote Blob fixtures. Earlier real local Blob optimizer proof and its distinction from the canonical store's missing object remain in the preserved prior report.
