+- Isolated E2E media storage and telemetry from inherited `.env.local` values. The harness explicitly blanks remote media credentials and production target flags while retaining development Clerk authentication and its disposable local database. Three regressions exercise actual backend selection with inherited Blob/Supabase settings.
+- Changed the Blob-backed managed-media route to stream the official SDK's public read response. The former 307 redirect caused the internal Next.js image optimizer to fail. Five regressions cover image bytes, metadata, conditional status and generic missing/unavailable responses. Only selected public metadata is forwarded. The local backend behavior remains unchanged. A real read-only local Next.js probe returned 200 for the existing public Blob image (179,963 bytes) and its optimized result (20,710 bytes), with no redirects or writes. The same Blob-only object is absent from the canonical deployment’s distinct configured store (`NoSuchKey`), so that anonymous response does not establish a production optimizer defect.
+
+Before the harness isolation repair, one synthetic admin upload reached the configured remote Blob store. Its exact key, timestamp, size and type were verified, then that fixture alone was deleted. Authoritative listing returned zero exact matches and the SDK reported it absent. The cleanup record remains in the locally retained machine receipts. The configured store was not established to be the canonical production store; no real media was modified.
+
+## Verification
+
+The final source passes 370 unit tests with no skips and 100% reported line, branch and function coverage, lint/type checks, formatting, dead-code checking, the configured production audit gate and the production build. Public functional browser tests pass 190 cases with 22 declared skips; both authenticated admin cases pass. Strict HTML separately fails ten Clerk attributes across 408 server/hydrated documents for 204 routes. The evidence JSON retains the terminal receipts. The suite covers desktop/mobile Chromium, Firefox/WebKit smoke journeys, authenticated admin creation/update/deletion and uploads, database access boundaries, no-JavaScript navigation, public-resource isolation, and strict server/hydrated HTML across every sitemap route plus sign-in/sign-up.
+
+Public functional tests and admin tests were invoked separately because Playwright's dependency gate would otherwise prevent admin tests from running after the known strict HTML failure. The strict test ran separately with its original assertion intact. No validation errors were filtered, no widget DOM was rewritten, and no source test assertion was weakened.
+
+The installed Docker Desktop service was started for the existing disposable PostgreSQL test harness and restored to its original inactive state after confirming zero other containers remained. The harness removes only its own database container/network/volumes and synthetic local uploads. Its development-only Clerk fixtures use suppressed-delivery test addresses and their existing cleanup hooks. External database or authentication production credentials were not used for mutation tests.
+
+## Historical strict HTML baseline
+
+Nu HTML Checker rejects ten generic `span` elements carrying `aria-label`: five social provider icons on each hydrated Clerk sign-in/sign-up page. All belong to vendor UI; no owned page HTML errors remain. The latest compatible Next SDK does not fix this. The test loads the current ClerkJS 6.40.1 and UI 1.41.2.
+
+Official [current ProviderIcon source](https://github.com/clerk/javascript/blob/main/packages/ui/src/common/ProviderIcon.tsx) unconditionally assigns `aria-label` to `Span` without an explicit image role. [Clerk PR #9897](https://github.com/clerk/javascript/pull/9897) adds `aria-hidden` to decorative social icons but leaves that label; the failing markup has both attributes. The [latest UI changelog](https://github.com/clerk/javascript/blob/main/packages/ui/CHANGELOG.md) and latest compatible SDK changelog were inspected.
+
+Supported [appearance options](https://clerk.com/docs/nextjs/guides/customizing-clerk/appearance-prop/options) change layout; [appearance elements](https://clerk.com/docs/nextjs/guides/customizing-clerk/appearance-prop/bring-your-own-css) accept styles/classes. Neither provides a provider-icon render or attribute replacement slot. Switching button variants uses the same icon component. [Clerk Elements](https://clerk.com/docs/guides/customizing-clerk/elements/overview) is deprecated and Core 2 only. The supported alternative is a complete custom authentication flow using Core 3 hooks, which must separately preserve all configured providers, sign-up transfer, verification, recovery, factors and session tasks; it is not an equivalent icon template replacement. The current built-in sign-in and successful admin journeys were preserved. Strict HTML acceptance was open at this baseline; the later bundled UI report records the narrow package fix and passing HTML check.
+
+## Production and account gates
+
+The canonical deployment is `dpl_B5ca2VsdMDTn4aJKWgTL51mpmdtz`, READY, with Vercel Git revision `130686f829331bfc603ba128582cdac1361984aa`, at [roobsworld.com](https://roobsworld.com). The local `fresh-start` HEAD has that same revision, with additional uncommitted changes. Git revision metadata alone does not attest that the deployed bundle matches the current dirty worktree. These local fixes have no new production delivery receipt.
+
+Anonymous `/`, `/about`, `/projects`, `/blog`, `/contact` and `/sign-in` returned 200. Anonymous `/api/health` returned the intentionally concealed 404. This is not evidence of a readiness failure.
+
+Historical [GitHub Actions run 39895073524](https://github.com/Grindhead/Portfolio/actions/runs/39895073524) ended `startup_failure` with zero jobs/check runs. A later minimal runner diagnostic also ended before any job started. No account or CI settings were changed by this verification.
+
+Creating a Portfolio-project-only Vercel token with a 90-day expiry through `POST /v3/user/tokens?teamId=team_Q3bXo2jPBKdjs3tuoKLtqUe2` returned **403 Forbidden**. No token was created and no GitHub secret was changed. The protected Production `VERCEL_TOKEN` metadata remains dated 2 October 2026. Existing CLI access uses short-lived OAuth and is not a durable CI credential replacement. Account reauthentication or a properly authorized project-scoped CI token is required.
+
+The owner-authorized canonical protected-health check returned HTTP 200 with database and storage OK at `2026-10-24T04:44:25.543Z`, revision `130686f829331bfc603ba128582cdac1361984aa`. This verifies the existing canonical deployment only; the new candidate is not deployed, and its hosted CI, staged readiness and promotion acceptance remain pending.
+
+The [production runbook](deployment-runbook.md) explicitly makes the CI-gated GitHub Actions workflow the only production promotion authority. Therefore direct CLI promotion was not used to bypass the account failure. Completing delivery requires the account owner to restore permitted Actions execution, provide the valid protected Vercel CI credential, and then let the current protected workflow stage, verify and promote the exact reviewed revision.
+
