-      "requirements": "# First-run loading investigation — 8 October 2026\n\nThe hosted build `1.6.6-dev.26273225354` returned to Jest's home page before an\ninteractive screen appeared. Unity diagnostics remained at `process_start` for\nabout 12 seconds. The player and chat APIs returned 200 in that attempt; the\nreported `ERR_HTTP2_PROTOCOL_ERROR` was not reproduced.\n\nJest documents a 15-second timeout in Manual loading mode when no progress\nupdate arrives. The old template updated only Unity's local download bar. The\nC# Jest heartbeat starts after SDK initialization, leaving Unity downloads and\nearly scene startup uncovered. This gap is consistent with the observed exit,\nbut the platform did not report an explicit timeout reason. The cause of any\nremaining Unity startup stall still needs a hosted candidate trace.\n\n## Change\n\n- FitWindow loads a browser startup helper before Unity's loader. In embedded\n  Jest sessions (`sid` or test-console `tce=1`), it initializes the official SDK\n  with automatic login reminders disabled and repeats zero loading progress\n  every five seconds after SDK readiness.\n- The Unity SDK loader shares the helper's download promise. Slow SDK delivery\n  cannot cause two independent script loads to replace the SDK singleton.\n- The first successful C# progress update stops the browser heartbeat. Runtime\n  progress and the existing C# heartbeat then own loading updates.\n- The helper never calls `markGameLoaded`. First usable interaction, including\n  the first-run privacy overlay, retains the existing completion boundary.\n- Page exit, SDK failures, Unity initialization failure, and a two-minute boot\n  deadline stop the helper. Direct-host and unrelated embedded pages skip it.\n- `LaunchState` records local-data and launch-screen stages before its early\n  awaits, so a remaining stall can be located more precisely.\n\nOfficial behavior: [Unity loading screen](https://docs.jest.com/sdk/unity/loading-screen),\n[HTML5 loading screen](https://docs.jest.com/sdk/html5/loading-screen).\n\n## Local verification\n\nFrom the repository root:\n\n```bash\nnode Tools/JestSandboxValidation/tests/jest-loading-bootstrap.test.mjs\n```\n\nTen tests passed. They execute the actual template helper and WebGL bridges,\ncovering a simulated 45-second cold boot, SDK download races and failures,\nlate SDK readiness after runtime takeover, page exit, context gating, and the\nbounded boot deadline. The clock and platform calls are simulated; this is not\na real-device or hosted Unity gameplay pass.\n\nUnity MCP refreshed the source and ran the `JestPlatformServiceTests` and\n`LoadingServiceTests` groups in PlayMode: 44 passed, zero failed. These groups\ncontain no EditMode tests.\n\nThe build-only Jest WebGL player compiled and linked successfully. Inspection\nof the generated player confirmed the template helper matches its source and\nthe framework includes SDK download sharing and runtime takeover. Output:\n`Builds/PuzzlePunks_WebGL_JestLightweight`, version `1.6.6`.\n\nRelease validation then stopped packaging because estimated initial loading\nexceeded the existing strict 20 MiB budget:\n\n| Component | MiB |\n|---|---:|\n| Loader and framework | 0.09 |\n| Wasm | 8.33 |\n| Data | 9.38 |\n| Startup deferred content | 3.48 |\n| Reported total | 21.29 |\n\nThe release ZIP was not produced. The compiled player is available for local\ninspection but is not an accepted release candidate. The size limit was kept.\nUnity restored the original defines, product version, and template; the editor\nreturned to idle without compilation failures. No ProjectSettings source\nchanges remain from this build.\n\n## Hosted acceptance\n\n1. Use the owning team's developer account to confirm the active build and\n   loading-screen mode. Pin the test route to the candidate version ID.\n2. Use a fresh browser profile or clear only the candidate game's cached assets\n   and local game data. Test new guests, returning guests, and registered users.\n3. Throttle downloads so Unity startup exceeds 15 seconds. Confirm progress\n   updates continue during downloads and stop using the browser helper when\n   C# takes over. Keep logs without session tokens or signed player payloads.\n4. Confirm the first-run privacy screen accepts input and loading finishes at\n   that boundary. Complete onboarding and enter a match.\n5. Repeat with a warm cache, interrupted downloads, and failed SDK delivery.\n   Check physical iOS Safari and Android Chrome as well as desktop browsers.\n6. If Unity still stalls, use the new startup stages to distinguish local-data\n   work, launch-screen loading, and later service initialization. Capture the\n   platform's exit reason before assigning the cause to the chat stream.\n\nThe connected Jest account currently exposes only RoobsWorld and rejects\nPuzzle Punks with `Missing 'games:read' permission for this game.` The live fix\nand cold/warm hosted acceptance have not been verified. On 8 October, the\ncurrent in-app browser sandbox session also returned a 403 `Game not live yet`\npage before Unity boot. That session cannot verify the first-run freeze; the\nowning account must distinguish its access restrictions from game visibility.\nNo build was published or activated by this investigation.\n\n## Local recheck on 10 October 2026\n\nThe standard `BatchBuild_WebGL_JestLightweight` BuildOnly profile compiled and\nlinked again, then failed the unchanged strict 20 MiB packaging gate. The current\ninitial load is 21.06 MiB: loader 0.02 MiB, framework 0.07 MiB, Wasm 8.31 MiB,\ndata 9.17 MiB and startup deferred content 3.48 MiB. No accepted release ZIP was\nproduced and hosted acceptance remains open.\n\nAn earlier recheck reported 20.36 MiB while recovering temporary optimization\nbackups. Its startup bundles differ from the current candidate, so it does not\nestablish the size of this standard-profile build. A release compiler experiment\nalso reported 21.06 MiB and was reverted.\n\nSeven new metadata-restoration regressions and fourteen existing Unity EditMode\nchecks passed. Resources backup restoration now preserves the original GUID when\nreplacement metadata has a different identity and keeps layered authored metadata\nwith the same identity. The fresh guarded build compared 21,438 source files.\nAfter restoring the exact saved ProjectSettings and two generated SDK metadata\nfiles, no source additions, missing files or changed source hashes remained.\nThe Jest SDK recreates its `init.jspre` asset during preprocessing; preserving\nauthored Resources metadata is a separate check.\n\nCurrent build log SHA-256:\n`8fda812b1ce4fe4da1bd935ed86d5f6e03156fce9cf4e1f04f50ff95bb68ca47`.\nThe next gate is to reduce the standard-profile initial load below 20 MiB while\npreserving required startup content, then package and verify the exact candidate.\n"
+      "title": "First-run loading investigation \u2014 8 October 2026",
+      "requirements": "# First-run loading investigation \u2014 8 October 2026\n\nThe hosted build `1.6.6-dev.26273225354` returned to Jest's home page before an\ninteractive screen appeared. Unity diagnostics remained at `process_start` for\nabout 12 seconds. The player and chat APIs returned 200 in that attempt; the\nreported `ERR_HTTP2_PROTOCOL_ERROR` was not reproduced.\n\nJest documents a 15-second timeout in Manual loading mode when no progress\nupdate arrives. The old template updated only Unity's local download bar. The\nC# Jest heartbeat starts after SDK initialization, leaving Unity downloads and\nearly scene startup uncovered. This gap is consistent with the observed exit,\nbut the platform did not report an explicit timeout reason. The cause of any\nremaining Unity startup stall still needs a hosted candidate trace.\n\n## Change\n\n- FitWindow loads a browser startup helper before Unity's loader. In embedded\n  Jest sessions (`sid` or test-console `tce=1`), it initializes the official SDK\n  with automatic login reminders disabled and repeats zero loading progress\n  every five seconds after SDK readiness.\n- The Unity SDK loader shares the helper's download promise. Slow SDK delivery\n  cannot cause two independent script loads to replace the SDK singleton.\n- The first successful C# progress update stops the browser heartbeat. Runtime\n  progress and the existing C# heartbeat then own loading updates.\n- The helper never calls `markGameLoaded`. First usable interaction, including\n  the first-run privacy overlay, retains the existing completion boundary.\n- Page exit, SDK failures, Unity initialization failure, and a two-minute boot\n  deadline stop the helper. Direct-host and unrelated embedded pages skip it.\n- `LaunchState` records local-data and launch-screen stages before its early\n  awaits, so a remaining stall can be located more precisely.\n\nOfficial behavior: [Unity loading screen](https://docs.jest.com/sdk/unity/loading-screen),\n[HTML5 loading screen](https://docs.jest.com/sdk/html5/loading-screen).\n\n## Local verification\n\nFrom the repository root:\n\n```bash\nnode Tools/JestSandboxValidation/tests/jest-loading-bootstrap.test.mjs\n```\n\nTen tests passed. They execute the actual template helper and WebGL bridges,\ncovering a simulated 45-second cold boot, SDK download races and failures,\nlate SDK readiness after runtime takeover, page exit, context gating, and the\nbounded boot deadline. The clock and platform calls are simulated; this is not\na real-device or hosted Unity gameplay pass.\n\nUnity MCP refreshed the source and ran the `JestPlatformServiceTests` and\n`LoadingServiceTests` groups in PlayMode: 44 passed, zero failed. These groups\ncontain no EditMode tests.\n\nThe build-only Jest WebGL player compiled and linked successfully. Inspection\nof the generated player confirmed the template helper matches its source and\nthe framework includes SDK download sharing and runtime takeover. Output:\n`Builds/PuzzlePunks_WebGL_JestLightweight`, version `1.6.6`.\n\nRelease validation then stopped packaging because estimated initial loading\nexceeded the existing strict 20 MiB budget:\n\n| Component | MiB |\n|---|---:|\n| Loader and framework | 0.09 |\n| Wasm | 8.33 |\n| Data | 9.38 |\n| Startup deferred content | 3.48 |\n| Reported total | 21.29 |\n\nThe release ZIP was not produced. The compiled player is available for local\ninspection but is not an accepted release candidate. The size limit was kept.\nUnity restored the original defines, product version, and template; the editor\nreturned to idle without compilation failures. No ProjectSettings source\nchanges remain from this build.\n\n## Hosted acceptance\n\n1. Use the owning team's developer account to confirm the active build and\n   loading-screen mode. Pin the test route to the candidate version ID.\n2. Use a fresh browser profile or clear only the candidate game's cached assets\n   and local game data. Test new guests, returning guests, and registered users.\n3. Throttle downloads so Unity startup exceeds 15 seconds. Confirm progress\n   updates continue during downloads and stop using the browser helper when\n   C# takes over. Keep logs without session tokens or signed player payloads.\n4. Confirm the first-run privacy screen accepts input and loading finishes at\n   that boundary. Complete onboarding and enter a match.\n5. Repeat with a warm cache, interrupted downloads, and failed SDK delivery.\n   Check physical iOS Safari and Android Chrome as well as desktop browsers.\n6. If Unity still stalls, use the new startup stages to distinguish local-data\n   work, launch-screen loading, and later service initialization. Capture the\n   platform's exit reason before assigning the cause to the chat stream.\n\nThe connected Jest account currently exposes only RoobsWorld and rejects\nPuzzle Punks with `Missing 'games:read' permission for this game.` The live fix\nand cold/warm hosted acceptance have not been verified. On 8 October, the\ncurrent in-app browser sandbox session also returned a 403 `Game not live yet`\npage before Unity boot. That session cannot verify the first-run freeze; the\nowning account must distinguish its access restrictions from game visibility.\nNo build was published or activated by this investigation.\n\n## Local recheck on 10 October 2026\n\nThe standard `BatchBuild_WebGL_JestLightweight` BuildOnly profile compiled and\nlinked again, then failed the unchanged strict 20 MiB packaging gate. The current\ninitial load is 21.06 MiB: loader 0.02 MiB, framework 0.07 MiB, Wasm 8.31 MiB,\ndata 9.17 MiB and startup deferred content 3.48 MiB. No accepted release ZIP was\nproduced and hosted acceptance remains open.\n\nAn earlier recheck reported 20.36 MiB while recovering temporary optimization\nbackups. Its startup bundles differ from the current candidate, so it does not\nestablish the size of this standard-profile build. A release compiler experiment\nalso reported 21.06 MiB and was reverted.\n\nSeven new metadata-restoration regressions and fourteen existing Unity EditMode\nchecks passed. Resources backup restoration now preserves the original GUID when\nreplacement metadata has a different identity and keeps layered authored metadata\nwith the same identity. The fresh guarded build compared 21,438 source files.\nAfter restoring the exact saved ProjectSettings and two generated SDK metadata\nfiles, no source additions, missing files or changed source hashes remained.\nThe Jest SDK recreates its `init.jspre` asset during preprocessing; preserving\nauthored Resources metadata is a separate check.\n\nCurrent build log SHA-256:\n`8fda812b1ce4fe4da1bd935ed86d5f6e03156fce9cf4e1f04f50ff95bb68ca47`.\nThe next gate is to reduce the standard-profile initial load below 20 MiB while\npreserving required startup content, then package and verify the exact candidate.\n"
