       "source": "docs/HANDOFF.md",
       "title": "The Hive: hand-off guide",
-      "requirements": "# The Hive: hand-off guide\n\nThis guide is for the people who will run The Hive day to day, and for the developer who looks after it. Sections marked **(technical)** are for developers. Everything else is written for non-developers.\n\nThe project folder is `BeeHive`, and some files still say \"Buzz TV\". Buzz TV was the working name. The product is **The Hive**.\n\nWebsite wording now has a Google Sheet mapping. See [Website text in Google Sheets](WEBSITE-TEXT.md) for the Sheet, editing instructions and deployment settings. Once connected in production, Sheet wording takes precedence over the original question text edited in admin; admin still manages questions, answer positions and scoring.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [What it is](#1-what-it-is)\n2. [How it works](#2-how-it-works)\n3. [Day-to-day admin](#3-day-to-day-admin)\n4. [Putting it in Moodle or Totara](#4-putting-it-in-moodle-or-totara)\n5. [Running the Hive TV in the building](#5-running-the-hive-tv-in-the-building)\n6. [The real logo and the Gräbenbach fonts](#6-the-real-logo-and-the-gräbenbach-fonts)\n7. [Technical reference (technical)](#7-technical-reference-technical)\n8. [Known issues and next steps](#8-known-issues-and-next-steps)\n9. [The AI agents (Claude)](#9-the-ai-agents-claude)\n\n---\n\n## 1. What it is\n\nThe Hive is a short, playful learning game for Factory International's learning hub (Titus, which runs on Moodle). The home page shows three stages: **Build Your Bee**, **Discover Your Transferable Skills**, and **Join The Hive**. A learner starts with a bee and an editable, made-up name, then selects **Next** to open the first question of the 15-question career profile. Questions appear one at a time, with no category choice and no right or wrong answers. A cartoon bee hosts the quiz from a retro TV and grows as they answer. When they finish, their bee flies off to join **the Hive**, a live swarm shown full screen on a TV in the building. Learners should invent bee names rather than use their real names. Moodle does not send learner details to the Hive.\n\n| Page | Address | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| The game | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app | What learners play. This is what Moodle shows. |\n| The Hive TV | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/tv | The live swarm, for the screen in the building. |\n| Admin | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/admin | Where approved staff edit questions, hide bees and see stats. |\n\n### 1.1 How each bee gets its look\n\n**Decision (8 October 2026):** the home page's **Build Your Bee** stage lets learners name their starter bee. Each bee's appearance still grows from quiz answers, and its traits relate to what the questions were about. The earlier costume builder, unlockable skins and Random Bee option remain removed.\n\nThe bee grows on the TV while the learner plays: each answer can add or change a trait, with a little animation (none if motion is paused or reduced). The results screen shows the finished bee with one line explaining it, for example *\"Your headset mic comes from your Events & Live answers, the megaphone from Marketing, Content & Communications and your wavy stripes from communication & storytelling.\"*\n\n| Career pathway | Costume | Prop (when it's the second pathway) | Wing pattern |\n|**Results screen extras.** A gentle honeycomb confetti plays when results appear (none if motion is paused or reduced). **Save my bee** downloads a square PNG card of the learner's bee with its name, its top pathway (or arts score) and the Factory International logo. The card is drawn in the learner's browser and never uploaded.\n\n**Carrying on later.** A quiz in progress is kept in the learner's browser for 2 hours, so closing the window (or relaunching from Moodle) on the same device offers \"Carry on where you left off\".\n\n---|---|---|---|\n| Film & TV Production | Director's cap | Clapperboard | Film frames |\n| Events & Live | Headset mic | Crew lanyard | Stage lights |\n| Marketing, Content & Communications | Speech bubble | Megaphone | Rays |\n| Design | Beret | Paintbrush | Brushstroke |\n| Music | Headphones | Music notes | Notes |\n| Theatre & Performance | Theatre mask | Tragedy mask | Curtain |\n| Digital & Creative Technology | VR goggles | Circuit chip | Circuit |\n\n- **Career quiz:** the **strongest pathway** gives the costume and wing pattern, the **second pathway** gives the prop, the **top strength** gives the stripe pattern (for example wavy for Communication & storytelling, pixel for Digital curiosity), and having two or three strengths adds a glow.\n- **Arts quizzes:** the quiz gives the look (Theatre: mask and spotlight; Music: headphones and notes; Dance: tutu and ribbon; Visual Art: beret and palette). The score only adds **polish**: more stripes and more sparkle or glow for more right answers. There is never a \"bad\" bee.\n- Every bee has a Hive Yellow (#FFE500) body with black, white and Machine Green details, in line with the brand.\n- The home page suggests a made-up name such as \"Encore Buzz\". Learners can edit it before selecting **Next**. Names must have 2 to 24 Unicode characters, start with a letter, and use letters, digits, spaces, apostrophes or hyphens. The server checks the format and blocked words before the quiz starts and again before publication.\n- The learner's device remembers its bee and edit key. Playing again on that device can rename and regrow the same bee. The Hive TV updates existing bees' names and designs.\n- **It can't be faked.** The server works the bee out from the answers itself (and checks arts answers against the questions) before saving it. The same answers always give the same bee.\n- Bees made with the old builder before 8 October 2026 still show on the Hive TV as they were.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How it works\n\nThere are three parts.\n\n- **Vercel** hosts the website: the game, the Hive TV, the admin pages, and a small server program (the \"API\") that the pages talk to.\n- **Supabase** is the database. It stores the quizzes and questions, the bees, an anonymous record of each finished quiz (\"plays\"), and the list of admins. Supabase also sends the admin login emails.\n- **The SCORM package** is a tiny file you upload to Moodle. It doesn't contain the game. It shows the live website inside Moodle and passes the result back to Moodle.\n\nThe browser never talks to Supabase directly. Every request goes to the Vercel API, which checks it and then reads or writes the database using a secret key that only the server has.\n\n```text\n  Learner in Moodle                 Phone (QR code)        TV in the building\n  +---------------------+           +-------------+        +---------------+\n  | SCORM launcher      |           | the game    |        | /tv           |\n  |  +---------------+  |           +------+------+        +-------+-------+\n  |  | the game (/)  |  |                  |                       |\n  |  +-------+-------+  |                  |                       |\n  +----------|----------+                  |                       |\n   result -> |  (score / completed,        |                       |\n   Moodle    |   sent to the launcher)     |                       |\n             v                             v                       v\n  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |  Vercel: web pages + API (/api/...)                                     |\n  |  checks every request, scores the career quiz, rate-limits              |\n  +--------------------+-------------------------------+--------------------+\n                       | secret key (server only)      | optional\n                       v                               v\n  +-------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+\n  |  Supabase                           |   |  Anthropic (Claude)        |\n  |  questions, bees, plays, admins     |   |  invents \"AI bees\" while   |\n  |  + admin login emails               |   |  the Hive is quiet         |\n  +-------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+\n\n  Admin (/admin) -> Vercel API -> Supabase   (email one-time link to log in)\n```\n\nSome useful consequences:\n\n- **Editing a question in admin changes the live game straight away.** You never need to re-upload anything to Moodle.\n- **AI bees.** While fewer than 30 real bees are in the Hive, the Hive TV asks Claude (an AI) to invent a few arts-themed bees so the screen never looks empty. They are labelled \"AI\" in admin. This only happens while the Hive TV page is open and an Anthropic key is set (see [Known issues](#8-known-issues-and-next-steps)). Once 30 real bees are visible, AI bees stop being shown.\n- **Learners' bees.** Learners can keep the suggested made-up name or invent their own. The server validates and moderates the name before the quiz and before saving the bee. The learner's device remembers its bee, so playing again updates the same bee instead of adding a new one.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Day-to-day admin\n\n### 3.1 Logging in\n\n1. Go to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/admin.\n2. Enter your work email and select **Send login link**.\n3. Open the email and click the link. It brings you back to the admin page, logged in.\n\nOnly emails on the admin list can log in. If your email isn't on the list, the page says the same thing (\"a login link is on its way\") but no email is sent. This is deliberate, so nobody can find out who is on the list.\n\nGood to know:\n\n- Check your spam folder if the email doesn't arrive.\n- There is a limit of 5 login requests per 10 minutes from one network. Wait a few minutes if you see \"Too many requests\".\n- Your login lasts for that browser tab. Closing the tab logs you out.\n\n### 3.2 Who can do what\n\nThere are two roles.\n\n| Task | Editor | Owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| Add, edit and delete questions | Yes | Yes |\n| Create and edit quizzes (\"disciplines\"), show or hide them | Yes | Yes |\n| See the Hive tab and Stats | Yes | Yes |\n| Delete a whole quiz | No | Yes |\n| Hide a bee from the Hive TV | No | Yes |\n| Add and remove admins | No | Yes |\n| Delete scores older than 12 months | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.3 Adding and removing admins (owners only)\n\n1. Open the **Admins** tab.\n2. Under **Add someone**, enter the email, choose **Editor** or **Owner**, and select **Add**. They can log in straight away.\n3. To change someone's role, add their email again with the new role.\n4. To remove someone, select **Remove** next to their email, then select it again to confirm.\n\nYou can't remove or demote yourself. The database also prevents removal or demotion of the final owner, including concurrent requests. On 10 October 2026, the owner confirmed that `grindheadgames@gmail.com` should remain the owner; no additional address was supplied.\n\n### 3.4 Editing the arts quizzes\n\nThe arts quizzes are Theatre, Music, Dance and Visual Art. They remain editable in admin and available through the API. The learner website opens the transferable skills quiz directly. An arts round requested through the API contains up to 10 questions picked at random.\n\n1. Open the **Quizzes** tab and pick the quiz from the list on the left. Its details appear first.\n2. Select **Edit questions**, then select a question to open it. To add one, select **+ Add question**.\n3. Fill in:\n   - **Question**: up to 300 characters.\n   - **Answers**: 2 to 4. Leave a box empty to use fewer than 4.\n   - **The correct answer**: tick the circle next to it.\n   - **Fact shown after answering**: optional, up to 300 characters. Learners see it after each answer.\n   - **Review status**: Draft or Approved (see 3.6).\n4. Select **Save**. An \"Unsaved\" label shows until you do.\n\nTo delete a question, open it, select **Delete**, then select it again to confirm.\n\nSelect **Close questions** to return to the quiz details. Save any edits first, or confirm that you want to discard them. Selecting another quiz also closes the question list.\n\nEach arts quiz should have at least 10 questions. The list on the left shows how many each one has. An active quiz with fewer than 10 questions remains available through the API, but its rounds will be shorter.\n\n**Quiz settings.** Select **Settings** beside the quiz name to change the quiz name, blurb, list order and **Show to learners**. Untick **Show to learners** to disable public API access to a quiz without deleting it. Disabling **Discover Your Transferable Skills** prevents learners from starting the website's quiz. The short code can't be changed after the quiz is created. Changes to the public API quiz list can take up to a minute to appear.\n\n### 3.5 Editing the career quiz (Discover Your Transferable Skills)\n\nThis quiz has no right answers. Learners answer every question, in the order shown in admin. Each answer carries hidden \"tags\" that the server uses to work out the learner's result. Learners never see the tags.\n\n**How the result is worked out.** For each answer the learner picks:\n\n- its **main pathway** gets 2 points;\n- each of its **other pathways** gets 1 point;\n- each of its **roles** gets 1 point, counted under the main pathway;\n- each of its **strengths** gets 1 point.\n\nThe result shows the pathway with the most points and 2 to 4 roles to explore in it, a second pathway, and the learner's top 3 strengths. Ties go to the pathway higher up this list: Film & TV Production, Events & Live, Marketing Content & Communications, Design, Music, Theatre & Performance, Digital & Creative Technology. Learners can save or print their result, replay the same quiz, or watch the Hive TV.\n\nTo edit a question:\n\n1. Open the **Quizzes** tab and pick **Discover Your Transferable Skills**.\n2. Select **Edit questions**, then select a question to open it.\n3. Edit:\n   - **Scenario**: the first line, which sets the scene.\n   - **Question**: the question itself.\n   - **Answers A to E**: 2 to 5 answers. Clear an answer's text to remove it.\n   - For each answer:\n     - **Main pathway (2 points)**: required.\n     - **Also scores (1 point each)**: tick any other pathways.\n     - **Roles**: separated by commas, for example `Producer, Production Manager`.\n     - **Strengths**: separated by commas. The box suggests strengths already used elsewhere, so spellings stay consistent. Up to 8 roles and 8 strengths per answer.\n   - **Review status**.\n4. Select **Save**.\n\n**Reorder** questions with the **↑** and **↓** arrows next to each one. Save or discard any open changes first.\n\nThe 7 pathways themselves and their descriptions are set in the code (`backend/hive/career.js`), not in admin. Ask a developer to change them. The API retains arts quiz pairings, but the learner results screen does not display them.\n\n### 3.6 Review status\n\nAll questions are **Approved** (live), and new questions start as Approved. You can still mark a question **Draft** as a reminder for your team while you rework it, but this is a checklist only: **learners see every question in a shown quiz, whatever its status.** Select **Edit questions** to use the review filters and search box. To keep a quiz away from learners until it is ready, untick **Show to learners** in its settings.\n\n### 3.7 Hiding a bee from the Hive TV (owners only)\n\n1. Open the **Hive** tab. It shows the newest 300 bees.\n2. Find the bee. You can filter by Learners, AI or Hidden.\n3. Select **Hide**. The bee leaves the Hive TV within a few seconds.\n4. To bring it back, filter by **Hidden** and select **Show**.\n\nLearners can type bee names, which the server checks against a blocked-words list. Use **Hide** if a learner name or an AI bee's personality line still needs removing from the TV.\n\n### 3.8 Reading the stats\n\nThe **Stats** tab covers the last 12 months:\n\n- **Rounds played**: how many quizzes were finished.\n- **Learner bees in the Hive**: real (not AI) bees that aren't hidden.\n- **Average score by discipline**: plays and average score for each arts quiz. The career quiz shows \"n/a\" because it has no score.\n- **Career quiz: top pathways**: how many learners got each pathway as their strongest result. These counts start from 7 October 2026, when this was added.\n\nAll stats are anonymous. They are not linked to any learner. Moodle keeps its own per-learner records (see section 4).\n\n### 3.8a Insights\n\nThe **Insights** tab shows real data only (nothing is made up or seeded), for the last 7, 30, 90 or 365 days:\n\n- **Tiles:** quizzes completed (career and arts) and new learner bees.\n- **Activity by day:** quizzes completed each day (bars) and new bees (dots).\n- **Career pathway spread:** each learner's strongest pathway.\n- **Average score by arts quiz.**\n- **Most-missed arts questions:** questions answered at least 3 times, ranked by the share of wrong answers, with the correct answer shown. A very high rate can mean a hard question, or a wrong or unclear answer, so it's worth a check. (This counts from 8 October 2026, when it was added.)\n- **Download CSV:** every finished quiz in the period (date, quiz, score, total, percent, top pathway). No names, emails or ids are included.\n\nWhile there's little data, each panel says so. Insights need an admin login and are limited to 30 refreshes a minute per admin.\n\n### 3.9 Deleting old data (owners only)\n\nLearners are told that scores are kept for 12 months. To keep that promise:\n\n1. Open the **Stats** tab.\n2. Under **Data retention**, select **Delete scores older than 12 months**, then select it again to confirm.\n\nA Supabase Cron job now runs daily at 03:15 UTC and deletes anonymous plays older than 365 days. The owner approved this schedule on 10 October 2026. The manual button remains available. The job preserves bees, questions and admins, and also removes expired rate-limit bookkeeping and its own job logs older than 30 days. Check the job in Supabase Cron if retention monitoring reports a failure.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Putting it in Moodle or Totara\n\n**Step-by-step guide, with recommended settings and troubleshooting: [docs/LMS-SETUP.md](LMS-SETUP.md).** This section is the summary.\n\nThere are two ways. The SCORM package is recommended because Moodle can then record completion and scores.\n\n### 4.1 Build the SCORM package (developer, about a minute)\n\nOn a computer with the project and Node.js 20 or newer:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build:scorm\n```\n\nThis writes `dist/the-hive-scorm12.zip` (SCORM 1.2). By default it points at https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app, and any finished quiz marks the activity **completed**.\n\nOptions:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build:scorm -- --pass 70                          # passed/failed at 70% on the arts quizzes\nnpm run build:scorm -- https://hive.example.org --pass 70  # a different web address\nnpm run build:scorm -- --title \"The Hive\"                  # the title Moodle shows\n```\n\nYou only need to rebuild if the web address, pass mark or title changes. Editing questions never needs a new package. The deploy script (section 7.5) also rebuilds it.\n\n### 4.2 Upload it to Moodle\n\nExact names vary a little between Moodle and Totara versions.\n\n1. In the course, turn editing on.\n2. Select **Add an activity or resource**, then **SCORM package**.\n3. Give it a name, and upload `the-hive-scorm12.zip` under **Package file**.\n4. **Appearance**:\n   - **Display package**: **Current window** works best. The game needs at least 640 pixels of height.\n   - Hide the course structure and navigation, as there is only one page.\n   - If you choose **New window**, make it at least 1000 × 750 pixels.\n5. **Attempts management**:\n   - **Number of attempts**: Unlimited.\n   - **Grading method**: Highest grade.\n   - **Force new attempt**: No. The package keeps the learner's best score in an attempt anyway.\n6. **Activity completion**: choose **Students must receive a status** and tick **Completed**. Learners currently play the career quiz, which always reports *Completed* (it has no score), so **don't require Passed**: nobody would ever complete the activity. (`--pass` only matters if the scored arts quizzes are offered to learners again.) Also set the SCORM **Height** to at least 700 pixels if you embed it in the current window, because the launcher needs about 670.\n7. Save, then try it as a student (or with \"Log in as\") before telling learners.\n\n### 4.3 What Moodle records\n\nFor an **arts quiz**:\n\n- **Score**: the percentage correct, 0 to 100. If the learner plays more than once, the best score is kept.\n- **Status**: **completed** after any finished quiz. With `--pass`, it is **passed** or **failed** instead.\n- **Interactions** (Moodle: the activity's **Reports**, then **Interactions**): one line per question, with the learner's answer, the correct answer and right or wrong.\n\nFor the **career quiz**:\n\n- **Status**: **completed**, with no score. A \"passed\" already recorded is kept.\n- **Interactions**: one line per question with the letter chosen (a to e), marked \"neutral\".\n- **Comments**: the learner's latest result, in the form \"Creative Career Discovery: *pathway* (*suggested role*). Strengths: *top 3 strengths*\".\n\nMoodle also records time spent. It does not send learner names or other Moodle details to the Hive. Learners can type their own bee names, so the game asks them to use made-up names.\n\nThe launcher has an **Open The Hive in a new window** link. Anything played in that separate window is **not** recorded in Moodle.\n\n### 4.4 The plain alternative: a link or an iframe\n\nYou can also link to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app with a Moodle **URL** resource, or embed it in a **Page** with an iframe, for example:\n\n```html\n<iframe src=\"https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/\" title=\"The Hive\" style=\"width:100%;height:800px;border:0\" allow=\"fullscreen\"></iframe>\n```\n\nThis is simpler, but **Moodle records nothing**: no scores, no completion beyond \"viewed\".\n\n### 4.5 Framing rules\n\n- The game is allowed to be shown inside other sites. The site's security settings say `frame-ancestors *`, which means any site may frame it. That is what lets Moodle and the SCORM launcher show it.\n- To allow only your Moodle, a developer can change `frame-ancestors *` in `vercel.json` to your Moodle address (for example `frame-ancestors https://learning.example.org`) and redeploy.\n- The admin pages can never be framed (`frame-ancestors 'none'` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY`). Always open admin in its own tab.\n- Inside Moodle, some browsers keep the game's storage separate from a direct visit. So a learner's bee may not be remembered between Moodle and a direct visit. Their bee still joins the Hive.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Running the Hive TV in the building\n\n### Set-up\n\n1. On the laptop connected to the TV, open **https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/tv** in Chrome or Edge.\n2. Go full screen. Press **F11** (or use your browser's full-screen option), or select the **Full screen** button in the corner. F11 is better for a screen left running, because it stays on after the page reloads itself (see below). The on-screen button's full screen ends when the page reloads.\n3. Turn off sleep and screen-saver in the laptop's power settings. The page also asks the browser to keep the screen awake, but not all browsers do this.\n\nThe mouse pointer and the corner buttons hide after 3 seconds without movement. Move the mouse to bring them back.\n\n### What's on screen\n\n- Every visible bee flying. New learner bees get a \"Just landed\" spotlight. In a busy Hive only the newest 24 learner bees are named.\n- A count of real bees (\"bees in the Hive\").\n- A scrolling strip with the web address, the latest bee names and the quiz names.\n- A **QR code** (\"Join the Hive\"). Scanning it opens the game on a phone. It points at the address the TV page was opened from, so open the TV from the address you want people to use.\n\n### Pause motion\n\nThe **Pause motion** button stops the swarm, the scrolling strip and the blinking. Select **Play motion** to start them again. This is an accessibility feature for people affected by movement. The setting is remembered on that laptop's browser. If the laptop's system is set to \"reduce motion\", the TV starts paused.\n\n### Daily reload\n\nThe page reloads itself once every 24 hours after it was opened. This picks up any new version of the site without anyone touching the laptop.\n\n### If the network drops\n\nThe bees already on screen keep flying. After two failed checks, \"Reconnecting…\" appears at the bottom. The page keeps retrying, at most every 30 seconds, and carries on by itself when the connection returns. If the page has gone completely blank, reload it (F5).\n\nIf the database (Supabase) is down, the TV behaves the same way: it keeps the bees it has and shows \"Reconnecting…\".\n\n---\n\n## 6. The real logo and the Gräbenbach fonts\n\n### 6.1 The Factory International logo\n\nThe **Third Party Use** version of the Factory International logo is live (8 October 2026). It was taken unaltered, as vector artwork, from the brand guidelines PDF (*01 Guidelines_3rd party*, page 8). It was not redrawn.\n\n- `public/img/fi-logo-third-party-black.svg`: black, for light pages. It sits in the game's footer, beside the small print.\n- `public/img/fi-logo-third-party-white.svg`: white, for the dark Hive TV. It sits bottom left, above the ticker.\n- `public/img/logo.json` switches them on: `\"src\"` is used by the game and `\"srcOnDark\"` by the Hive TV. To turn the logo off, set both to `null` and deploy.\n\nThe guidelines' rules are built in:\n\n- **Clear space:** one fifth of the logo's width on every side. This is part of each SVG file's own frame, so no layout can crowd it.\n- **Minimum size:** 70 pixels wide for this version. The files are always shown at least 98 pixels wide, so the logo itself never goes below 70 pixels. It is about 86 pixels wide on the game on a desktop, 74 on a phone, and 70 to 93 on the Hive TV.\n- **Never over busy graphics:** it sits on plain white (game) or plain black (TV).\n\nIf Factory International sends updated files, replace the two SVGs and keep the same names, or update `logo.json`. If you change a file's name, use a new name rather than reusing an old one: Vercel's cache can briefly keep serving an old \"not found\" for a previously requested address.\n\n### 6.2 The Gräbenbach fonts\n\nFactory International's brand typefaces are Gräbenbach and Gräbenbach Mono (from Camelot Typefaces). **The web licence is confirmed (8 October 2026)**, so the fonts are on across the site. Arial, the guidelines' approved system font, is the fallback while the fonts load.\n\nHow the switch works today:\n\n- The font files are already in the project (`public/fonts/`, WOFF2 and WOFF, Latin subset).\n- The game and the TV use Gräbenbach **unless** the setting `BRAND_FONTS` is exactly `off`. If `BRAND_FONTS` is missing or has any other value, the fonts are on.\n- Technically, the pages start with the class `brand-fonts` on the `<html>` element, then remove it if the server says `BRAND_FONTS=off`.\n- The admin pages always use Gräbenbach, whatever the setting.\n\nTo switch them off for any reason, set `BRAND_FONTS` to `off` for Production in Vercel (or in `.env`, then run the deploy script) and redeploy. The font files stay published either way.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Technical reference (technical)\n\n### 7.1 What's in the project\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `backend/puzzle-punks/` | Separate Puzzle Punks Elixir repository, preserved during consolidation and excluded from the Hive deployment |\n| `public/` | The static site: `index.html` (game), `tv.html`, `admin.html`, `css/`, `js/`, `fonts/`, `img/` |\n| `api/index.js` | The single Vercel function. It re-exports `backend/hive/handler.js`. |\n| `backend/hive/handler.js` | Every API route, validation, rate limits, admin auth and AI bees |\n| `backend/hive/store.js` | Data layer: Supabase store, in-memory store, and the seed fallback |\n| `backend/hive/career.js` | Career pathways and scoring |\n| `backend/hive/seed-data.js` | Quiz content: the 4 arts quizzes and the career quiz |\n| `backend/hive/career-discovery-questions.json` | The client's career quiz (15 questions, 5 tagged answers each) |\n| `backend/hive/local.js` | Local dev server (`npm run dev`) |\n| `supabase/*.sql` | Schema, migrations and content seeds |\n| `scorm/` | SCORM 1.2 launcher templates |\n| `scripts/` | Deploy, SCORM build and content scripts |\n| `tests/` | Node test runner tests |\n\nThe server uses the official Anthropic SDK for optional AI features. It is plain Node 20+ with static HTML, CSS and JavaScript, and no build step for the site.\n\n### 7.2 Running locally\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev     # http://localhost:3000  (game /, Hive TV /tv, admin /admin)\n```\n\n- `npm run dev` explicitly opts into **in-memory** local storage when neither Supabase setting is present. Hosted deployments or partial credentials fail closed with 503. It is seeded with the bundled content and resets when you stop it.\n- In memory mode, admin login sends no email. Entering `grindheadgames@gmail.com` (the built-in owner) logs you straight in with a local dev token.\n- If a `.env` file exists with `SUPABASE_URL` and `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY`, the local server uses that Supabase project, which may be the live data. Be careful.\n- `PORT` and `HOST` change the port and address (default `127.0.0.1:3000`).\n\n### 7.3 Tests\n\n```bash\nnpm test\n```\n\nThis runs the Node test runner over `tests/*.test.js`, using the in-memory store. The tests cover:\n\n- the API, bees and admin roles (`api.test.js`);\n- question editing for both quiz types (`admin-edit.test.js`);\n- career content and scoring, stats and the logo switch (`career.test.js`);\n- the SCORM build (`scorm.test.js`);\n- same-site writes, headers, rate limits, the Supabase-down fallback and the CSP framing rules (`security.test.js`).\n\n### 7.4 Environment variables\n\nSet these in Vercel (**Project → Settings → Environment Variables**, Production) and, for local use, in `.env`. `.env.example` lists them. Never commit `.env`. Vercel only picks up changes on the next deploy.\n\n| Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `SUPABASE_URL` | The Supabase project URL, `https://<ref>.supabase.co`. With `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY`, it switches the app from memory to Supabase. |\n| `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY` | The Supabase secret key (`sb_secret_…`, or the legacy `service_role` key). **Server only.** It bypasses all database security. |\n| `SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | The publishable (or legacy `anon`) key. Used only by the server, for the admin login emails and token checks. |\n| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | Claude API key for the AI bees. If it is empty, there are no AI bees. |\n| `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` | Optional. Overrides the Claude model used by the AI agents and AI bees (the default is set in `backend/hive/agents.js`). |\n| `PUBLIC_URL` | Optional. The site's public address, used for the admin login link and by the deploy script. Set it when you move to a custom domain. |\n| `BRAND_FONTS` | `off` switches the Gräbenbach fonts off on the game and TV. Any other value, or none, leaves them on (section 6.2). |\n| `BLOCKED_WORDS` | Optional. Extra comma-separated words that learner bee names, AI bee names and AI personalities must not contain. A built-in list always applies. |\n\nUsed only by scripts on your own computer, never sent to Vercel:\n\n| Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` | A Supabase personal access token (`sbp_…`). `scripts/deploy-now.sh` uses it to read and update the Auth URLs, preserving existing redirect entries. |\n| `PORT`, `HOST` | Local dev server only. |\n\n### 7.5 Deploying\n\nThe site deploys to the Vercel project **buzz-tv**, which serves https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app. You need the Vercel CLI login (`npx vercel login`) and a `.env` file.\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/deploy-now.sh\n```\n\nWhat it does, in order:\n\n1. Loads `.env` and requires all three Supabase settings: URL, publishable key and secret key. Supply these before running; the helper does not fetch or save credentials.\n2. Checks that `.vercel/project.json` identifies **buzz-tv** in the expected Vercel team, then inspects that linked project.\n3. Replaces the Production Supabase settings and `PUBLIC_URL` with the supplied values. The public URL must use HTTPS and defaults to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app. `BRAND_FONTS` defaults to `on`.\n4. Updates optional AI, model, blocked-word and Google Sheets settings only when they are present in `.env`. Empty or absent optional values leave existing Vercel settings in place. To disable AI explicitly, remove the Production key in Vercel.\n5. If `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` is supplied, updates Supabase Auth's Site URL and adds `/admin` and `/admin/**` to its redirects, preserving other entries. Without it, configure the Auth URLs separately.\n6. Builds the SCORM package for the chosen public address, then deploys to the fixed **buzz-tv** project in the correct team.\n7. Checks the canonical health endpoint and fails unless Supabase storage reports healthy. Each failed command stops the helper. Output goes to the terminal; redirect it yourself if you need a log.\n\nAfter deploying, check that https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/api/health shows `\"storage\":\"supabase\"`. `\"ai\":true` means an Anthropic key is set.\n\n`scripts/deploy-wizard.sh` now uses the existing Buzz TV project and correct Vercel scope, runs the complete new-database migration order, defaults licensed fonts to on, preserves absent optional settings, and includes Sheets settings and both admin redirect patterns. `deploy-now.sh` uses the same fixed project, validates its link, fails on configuration errors, and preserves absent optional variables.\n\n### 7.6 Database set-up and migrations\n\nFor a new Supabase project:\n\n1. In the Supabase **SQL editor**, run the files in `supabase/` **in this order**:\n   1. `01_schema.sql`: tables, row-level security, and the first owner (`grindheadgames@gmail.com`).\n   2. `03_career_quiz.sql`: adds profile quizzes (`disciplines.mode`, `questions.scoring`, up to 5 answers).\n   3. `02_seed.sql`: the four arts quizzes (20 questions each). It only inserts into quizzes that have no questions yet.\n   4. `04_career_seed.sql`: the career quiz. **Re-running it deletes and replaces all career quiz questions**, including any edits made in admin.\n   5. `05_play_pathway.sql`: adds `plays.pathway` for the career stats.\n   6. `06_publish_all.sql`: marks every question approved (live) and makes that the default.\n   7. `07_play_answers.sql`: adds `plays.asked` and `plays.missed` (question ids only) for the most-missed questions in Insights.\n\n   8. `08_operational_safeguards.sql`: server-only shared rate limits and database protection for the final owner.\n   9. `09_retention_schedule.sql`: the approved daily 365-day purge and expired-limit cleanup.\n   10. `10_content_corrections.sql`: the six approved arts content corrections, matching only original fields.\n   11. `11_retry_safe_bees.sql`: atomically saves a bee and play with a durable hashed retry receipt.\n\n   `01_schema.sql` already includes the columns that `03`, `05` and `07` add, so on a new project those two make no changes. They are safe to re-run, and they are what upgrades an older database.\n2. In **Authentication → URL Configuration**, set **Site URL** to the live address and add `https://<address>/admin` to **Redirect URLs**. `deploy-now.sh` can do this for you.\n3. In **Project Settings → API Keys**, copy the project URL, the publishable key and a secret key into `.env` and Vercel.\n4. Deploy, then check `/api/health`.\n\nThe tables are `disciplines`, `questions`, `bees`, `plays` and `admins`. Admin logins also create users in Supabase Auth. Removing someone from the admin list blocks them, but it leaves their Auth user in place. Delete that in **Authentication → Users** if you want it gone.\n\n### 7.7 Content seed scripts\n\nThe quiz content in the code (`backend/hive/seed-data.js` and `backend/hive/career-discovery-questions.json`) is used three ways:\n\n- as the in-memory data for local dev and tests;\n- as the fallback content if Supabase is down (section 7.9);\n- as the source of the SQL seeds.\n\nOnce the site is live, **admin is the place to edit questions**. The scripts below are for set-up and for re-loading the client's career content.\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `npm run seed:sql` | Regenerates `supabase/02_seed.sql` and `supabase/04_career_seed.sql` from the code. |\n| `node scripts/push-career.js` | Pushes the career quiz from the code straight into the Supabase project in `.env`. It adds the new questions first, then removes the old ones, so the quiz is never left empty. **This overwrites any admin edits to the career quiz.** It needs `03_career_quiz.sql` applied first. |\n\n### 7.8 Security notes\n\n- **Database locked down.** Row-level security is on for all application tables, with no policies, and all rights are taken away from the `anon` and `authenticated` roles. A public key can read or write nothing. Only the server, with the secret key, can.\n- **The secret key is server-only.** It lives in Vercel's environment and `.env`. It never appears in the browser or the code. The browser only ever calls the site's own `/api/...`.\n- **Same-site JSON writes.** Every write must be sent as JSON (otherwise \"415\"). If a browser on another site tries it, it is refused (\"403\"). So other web pages can't post bees or plays. The API doesn't grant cross-site access (no CORS headers).\n- **Admin access.** Login uses Supabase Auth email one-time links. On every admin request the server checks the token with Supabase, then checks the email against the `admins` table and its role. The login reply is the same whether or not the email is on the list.\n- **Rate limits** (per network address):\n\n  | What | Limit |\n  |---|---|\n  | Admin login emails | 5 per 10 minutes |\n  | Saving a bee (new or updated) | 120 per minute (generous, as a whole office may share one address) |\n  | New bees | 60 per 10 minutes |\n  | Career quiz scoring | 40 per minute |\n  | AI bee requests | 4 per minute, plus at least 45 seconds between batches, 3 bees per batch, and at most 30 AI bees |\n\n- **Headers** (`vercel.json`):\n  - A strict Content Security Policy: only the site's own scripts, styles, fonts and connections.\n  - `frame-ancestors *` for the game and TV, so Moodle can frame them.\n  - `frame-ancestors 'none'` plus `X-Frame-Options: DENY` for admin.\n  - Also `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, a strict `Referrer-Policy`, a `Permissions-Policy` that blocks camera, microphone, location and payment, and `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy`.\n  - API responses also send `Cache-Control: no-store` (except the public lists below), `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`.\n- **Learner input is limited.**\n  - Bee names must have 2 to 24 Unicode characters, start with a letter, and use letters, digits, spaces, apostrophes or hyphens. `POST /api/bee-name` validates and moderates them before the quiz starts. Publication checks the name again.\n  - Bee designs must use the known options.\n  - Scores are range-checked.\n  - The career quiz tags never reach the browser, and the result is worked out on the server.\n  - AI bees are checked against the same design options, a name pattern and a blocked-words list.\n- **Learner privacy.** Plays are anonymous, and Moodle sends no learner details to the Hive. Bee names are public on the TV, so the game asks learners to invent names rather than use real names. User-entered names may still contain personal information. A random \"edit key\" on the learner's device lets them update their own bee. Only a hash of it is stored. Admin emails are stored in the `admins` table and Supabase Auth.\n- **Edge caching.** Vercel caches the quiz list and site config for up to 60 seconds, and the Hive for 2 seconds. This keeps the database quiet when many people play at once. Rounds, scoring and admin are never cached.\n\n### 7.9 If Supabase is down\n\n- **Learners can still read the quiz.** The quiz list and questions come from the content bundled in the code: the arts quizzes and the career quiz as in `backend/hive/seed-data.js`. Admin edits are **not** included. The score and publication endpoints require the shared limiter to be available, so the learner may need to retry when Supabase returns.\n- **Bees can't be saved.** At the end, the learner sees \"your bee couldn't reach the Hive\" with a **Try again** button. Their score is still shown, and still sent to Moodle.\n- **No stats are recorded** for those plays.\n- **The Hive TV** keeps the bees it has and shows \"Reconnecting…\".\n- **Admin is unavailable** (no login, no editing).\n- `/api/health` returns an error, so it is a quick way to check.\n\nThe fallback only applies to outages: network errors, Supabase server errors (5xx) and Supabase rate limiting (429). Other database errors still show as errors. Everything recovers by itself when Supabase returns.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Known issues and next steps\n\n### Content\n\n- **All content is live (8 October 2026).** Every question is approved and no \"draft\" labels are shown to learners.\n- **Arts content corrections completed, 10 October 2026.** The owner approved all six factual corrections in [docs/CONTENT-QA.md](CONTENT-QA.md). They are applied to the code, seed SQL and live questions.\n- Parts of the career quiz were written by us rather than supplied: the **strengths** tags, the **pathway descriptions** (`backend/hive/career.js`) and **Q15 answer E** (the client's document was cut off). Edit them if the client wants different wording. The API also retains arts quiz pairings written by us; these are not shown on the learner website.\n- The game's loading message names `buzz-tv-six.vercel.app`. Update it in `public/index.html` if the site moves to a new address.\n\n### Brand\n\n- **The supplied Factory International logo is live.** See section 6.1.\n- **Gräbenbach fonts:** licence confirmed and the fonts are live. See section 6.2.\n\n### Decisions log\n\n- **8 October 2026: bees grow from answers.** The costume builder and unlockable skins were removed. Bees are generated from quiz answers (section 1.1). The `skin` column on quizzes is no longer used.\n- **8 October 2026: automatic bee updates.** Removed the results-screen name selector. The live career bee and final result use the same pathway weights, tie rules and strengths, including when a learner changes an earlier answer.\n- **8 October 2026: welcome flow and custom names.** The home page shows **Build Your Bee**, **Discover Your Transferable Skills**, and **Join The Hive**, with an editable made-up name and a **Next** button. Names are validated and moderated before the quiz starts and before publication. Same-device replay can rename and regrow the existing bee, and the Hive TV updates its name and design.\n- **8 October 2026: direct learner quiz.** **Next** opens the first transferable skills question. The category chooser, category replay buttons and arts quiz suggestions were removed from the learner website. Admin editing and arts quiz APIs remain available.\n- **8 October 2026: content live.** All questions approved; no \"draft\" labels; Gräbenbach licence confirmed.\n\n### Technical\n\n- **AI bees are off.** No `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set in production (`/api/health` shows `\"ai\":false`). Without it, the Hive TV shows only learners' bees. AI bees are also only made while a Hive TV page is open.\n- **Rate limits are shared across production servers.** An atomic Supabase RPC stores hashed keys with short expiry. RLS and grants restrict it to the server. Expired bookkeeping is removed in bounded batches on requests and by the daily job. In-memory development retains local limits. An unavailable shared limiter does not bypass the cap.\n- **Career stats only count from 7 October 2026**, when the top pathway started being recorded. Career quizzes finished before then count as plays but have no pathway.\n- **The 365-day purge runs daily at 03:15 UTC**, with a manual admin control retained (section 3.9).\n- **Owner confirmed, 10 October 2026.** Keep `grindheadgames@gmail.com` as the owner, as requested.\n- **Owner protection is enforced by the API and database.** The API refuses self-removal and self-demotion. Database statement triggers serialize admin changes and refuse loss of the final owner, including bulk writes and truncation.\n- **Deployment scripts updated, 10 October 2026** (section 7.5).\n- **Live admin security headers verified, 10 October 2026.** The production page sends `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and a CSP with `frame-ancestors 'none'`.\n- **An active arts quiz with fewer than 10 questions remains available through the API**, with shorter rounds (section 3.4).\n- **Review status doesn't hide questions** from learners (section 3.6).\n\n---\n\n## 9. The AI agents (Claude)\n\nThe Hive has four optional AI features, written by Claude (Anthropic) through the official Anthropic SDK. **They are all off until an API key is added, and everything works without them**, using hand-written text instead.\n\n| Agent | Where | What it does | Without a key |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Host bee** | Hive TV | Greets each new bee in the \"Just landed\" banner, comments on what's happening (new arrivals, the most popular pathway today, today's top arts score) and shows a one-line \"state of the Hive\" under the title. | Hand-written greetings, comments and summary. |\n| **Career coach** | Career results | One personal paragraph and 2–3 next steps, based on the learner's strongest pathway, roles and strengths. Labelled \"Written for you by Claude, an AI coach\". | The pathway description and three hand-written next steps per pathway. |\n| **Draft with AI** | Admin > a quiz > Edit questions | Drafts one question (and, for the career quiz, its scoring tags) into a new, **unsaved** form. Nothing goes live until an admin checks it and selects Save. | The button explains that AI drafting is switched off. |\n| **AI bees** | Hive TV | Invents a few bees while the Hive has fewer than 30 real ones. | No AI bees. |\n\n### 9.1 Switching them on\n\n1. Create an API key in the Claude Console (platform.claude.com).\n2. In Vercel: **Project > Settings > Environment Variables**, add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Production (or add it to `.env` and run `scripts/deploy-now.sh`, which copies it to Vercel).\n3. Redeploy. To switch the agents off again, remove the variable and redeploy.\n\n`ANTHROPIC_MODEL` is optional: it overrides the default model set in `backend/hive/agents.js`.\n\n### 9.2 How they work (technical)\n\nAll the code and the exact prompts are in `backend/hive/agents.js` (AI bees: `askClaudeForBees` in `backend/hive/handler.js`). Every call:\n\n- runs **on the server only**: the browser never sees the key or talks to Anthropic;\n- uses low effort and the server-side **refusal fallback** (if Claude declines, another model tries; if that also declines, or the call fails or times out, the hand-written text is used);\n- checks the reply: lines must be short, family-friendly (the same word filter as bee names) and in the expected shape, or the hand-written text is used.\n\nPrompts, in short:\n\n- **Host bee:** \"You are the host bee on The Hive TV… warm, witty, family-friendly lines in UK English… never mention people, only bees by their bee names… don't invent facts beyond the snapshot.\" It sees only bee names, what they're wearing, counts, today's top pathway and top arts score.\n- **Career coach:** \"You are a warm, practical careers coach for the learning hub of Factory International… use only the profile you are given… do not invent specific jobs, vacancies, courses, programmes, events, people or contacts at Factory International or anywhere else… suggest general, realistic next steps.\" It sees only the quiz outcome: pathway names and descriptions, roles and strengths. **No personal data** (no name, bee name, email, IP address or Moodle details) is sent, and the server re-scores the answers itself, so only a genuine quiz result is used.\n- **Draft with AI:** \"You help staff at Factory International write questions… factually safe… must not make claims about Factory International itself.\" It sees the quiz name, the existing questions (to avoid repeats) and an optional topic. Drafts go through the same checks as hand-written questions and are marked Draft.\n\n### 9.3 Cost controls\n\n| Agent | Limits |\n|---|---|\n| Host bee | Refreshes at most every 3 minutes, and only when the Hive has changed (or every 15 minutes). At most 12 calls an hour per server. Replies are cached at Vercel's edge for 30 seconds, so many TVs share them. 20-second timeout. |\n| Career coach | One call per finished career quiz. 12 per network and 4 per browser tab every 10 minutes. 12-second timeout (the learner sees a loading state, then the hand-written steps if it's slow). |\n| Draft with AI | Admins only. 20 drafts an hour per admin. 30-second timeout. |\n| AI bees | Up to 3 bees per call, at most every 45 seconds, only while a Hive TV is open and there are fewer than 30 real bees. |\n\nAll calls use low effort. Coach, admin draft and AI seed request limits are shared in Supabase. Host cache and host refresh limits remain per instance.\n\n"
+      "requirements": "# The Hive: hand-off guide\n\nThis guide is for the people who will run The Hive day to day, and for the developer who looks after it. Sections marked **(technical)** are for developers. Everything else is written for non-developers.\n\nThe project folder is `BeeHive`, and some files still say \"Buzz TV\". Buzz TV was the working name. The product is **The Hive**.\n\nWebsite wording now has a Google Sheet mapping. See [Website text in Google Sheets](WEBSITE-TEXT.md) for the Sheet, editing instructions and deployment settings. Once connected in production, Sheet wording takes precedence over the original question text edited in admin; admin still manages questions, answer positions and scoring.\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [What it is](#1-what-it-is)\n2. [How it works](#2-how-it-works)\n3. [Day-to-day admin](#3-day-to-day-admin)\n4. [Putting it in Moodle or Totara](#4-putting-it-in-moodle-or-totara)\n5. [Running the Hive TV in the building](#5-running-the-hive-tv-in-the-building)\n6. [The real logo and the Gr\u00e4benbach fonts](#6-the-real-logo-and-the-gr\u00e4benbach-fonts)\n7. [Technical reference (technical)](#7-technical-reference-technical)\n8. [Known issues and next steps](#8-known-issues-and-next-steps)\n9. [The AI agents (Claude)](#9-the-ai-agents-claude)\n\n---\n\n## 1. What it is\n\nThe Hive is a short, playful learning game for Factory International's learning hub (Titus, which runs on Moodle). The home page shows three stages: **Build Your Bee**, **Discover Your Transferable Skills**, and **Join The Hive**. A learner starts with a bee and an editable, made-up name, then selects **Next** to open the first question of the 15-question career profile. Questions appear one at a time, with no category choice and no right or wrong answers. A cartoon bee hosts the quiz from a retro TV and grows as they answer. When they finish, their bee flies off to join **the Hive**, a live swarm shown full screen on a TV in the building. Learners should invent bee names rather than use their real names. Moodle does not send learner details to the Hive.\n\n| Page | Address | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| The game | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app | What learners play. This is what Moodle shows. |\n| The Hive TV | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/tv | The live swarm, for the screen in the building. |\n| Admin | https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/admin | Where approved staff edit questions, hide bees and see stats. |\n\n### 1.1 How each bee gets its look\n\n**Decision (8 October 2026):** the home page's **Build Your Bee** stage lets learners name their starter bee. Each bee's appearance still grows from quiz answers, and its traits relate to what the questions were about. The earlier costume builder, unlockable skins and Random Bee option remain removed.\n\nThe bee grows on the TV while the learner plays: each answer can add or change a trait, with a little animation (none if motion is paused or reduced). The results screen shows the finished bee with one line explaining it, for example *\"Your headset mic comes from your Events & Live answers, the megaphone from Marketing, Content & Communications and your wavy stripes from communication & storytelling.\"*\n\n| Career pathway | Costume | Prop (when it's the second pathway) | Wing pattern |\n|**Results screen extras.** A gentle honeycomb confetti plays when results appear (none if motion is paused or reduced). **Save my bee** downloads a square PNG card of the learner's bee with its name, its top pathway (or arts score) and the Factory International logo. The card is drawn in the learner's browser and never uploaded.\n\n**Carrying on later.** A quiz in progress is kept in the learner's browser for 2 hours, so closing the window (or relaunching from Moodle) on the same device offers \"Carry on where you left off\".\n\n---|---|---|---|\n| Film & TV Production | Director's cap | Clapperboard | Film frames |\n| Events & Live | Headset mic | Crew lanyard | Stage lights |\n| Marketing, Content & Communications | Speech bubble | Megaphone | Rays |\n| Design | Beret | Paintbrush | Brushstroke |\n| Music | Headphones | Music notes | Notes |\n| Theatre & Performance | Theatre mask | Tragedy mask | Curtain |\n| Digital & Creative Technology | VR goggles | Circuit chip | Circuit |\n\n- **Career quiz:** the **strongest pathway** gives the costume and wing pattern, the **second pathway** gives the prop, the **top strength** gives the stripe pattern (for example wavy for Communication & storytelling, pixel for Digital curiosity), and having two or three strengths adds a glow.\n- **Arts quizzes:** the quiz gives the look (Theatre: mask and spotlight; Music: headphones and notes; Dance: tutu and ribbon; Visual Art: beret and palette). The score only adds **polish**: more stripes and more sparkle or glow for more right answers. There is never a \"bad\" bee.\n- Every bee has a Hive Yellow (#FFE500) body with black, white and Machine Green details, in line with the brand.\n- The home page suggests a made-up name such as \"Encore Buzz\". Learners can edit it before selecting **Next**. Names must have 2 to 24 Unicode characters, start with a letter, and use letters, digits, spaces, apostrophes or hyphens. The server checks the format and blocked words before the quiz starts and again before publication.\n- The learner's device remembers its bee and edit key. Playing again on that device can rename and regrow the same bee. The Hive TV updates existing bees' names and designs.\n- **It can't be faked.** The server works the bee out from the answers itself (and checks arts answers against the questions) before saving it. The same answers always give the same bee.\n- Bees made with the old builder before 8 October 2026 still show on the Hive TV as they were.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How it works\n\nThere are three parts.\n\n- **Vercel** hosts the website: the game, the Hive TV, the admin pages, and a small server program (the \"API\") that the pages talk to.\n- **Supabase** is the database. It stores the quizzes and questions, the bees, an anonymous record of each finished quiz (\"plays\"), and the list of admins. Supabase also sends the admin login emails.\n- **The SCORM package** is a tiny file you upload to Moodle. It doesn't contain the game. It shows the live website inside Moodle and passes the result back to Moodle.\n\nThe browser never talks to Supabase directly. Every request goes to the Vercel API, which checks it and then reads or writes the database using a secret key that only the server has.\n\n```text\n  Learner in Moodle                 Phone (QR code)        TV in the building\n  +---------------------+           +-------------+        +---------------+\n  | SCORM launcher      |           | the game    |        | /tv           |\n  |  +---------------+  |           +------+------+        +-------+-------+\n  |  | the game (/)  |  |                  |                       |\n  |  +-------+-------+  |                  |                       |\n  +----------|----------+                  |                       |\n   result -> |  (score / completed,        |                       |\n   Moodle    |   sent to the launcher)     |                       |\n             v                             v                       v\n  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  |  Vercel: web pages + API (/api/...)                                     |\n  |  checks every request, scores the career quiz, rate-limits              |\n  +--------------------+-------------------------------+--------------------+\n                       | secret key (server only)      | optional\n                       v                               v\n  +-------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+\n  |  Supabase                           |   |  Anthropic (Claude)        |\n  |  questions, bees, plays, admins     |   |  invents \"AI bees\" while   |\n  |  + admin login emails               |   |  the Hive is quiet         |\n  +-------------------------------------+   +----------------------------+\n\n  Admin (/admin) -> Vercel API -> Supabase   (email one-time link to log in)\n```\n\nSome useful consequences:\n\n- **Editing a question in admin changes the live game straight away.** You never need to re-upload anything to Moodle.\n- **AI bees.** While fewer than 30 real bees are in the Hive, the Hive TV asks Claude (an AI) to invent a few arts-themed bees so the screen never looks empty. They are labelled \"AI\" in admin. This only happens while the Hive TV page is open and an Anthropic key is set (see [Known issues](#8-known-issues-and-next-steps)). Once 30 real bees are visible, AI bees stop being shown.\n- **Learners' bees.** Learners can keep the suggested made-up name or invent their own. The server validates and moderates the name before the quiz and before saving the bee. The learner's device remembers its bee, so playing again updates the same bee instead of adding a new one.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Day-to-day admin\n\n### 3.1 Logging in\n\n1. Go to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/admin.\n2. Enter your work email and select **Send login link**.\n3. Open the email and click the link. It brings you back to the admin page, logged in.\n\nOnly emails on the admin list can log in. If your email isn't on the list, the page says the same thing (\"a login link is on its way\") but no email is sent. This is deliberate, so nobody can find out who is on the list.\n\nGood to know:\n\n- Check your spam folder if the email doesn't arrive.\n- There is a limit of 5 login requests per 10 minutes from one network. Wait a few minutes if you see \"Too many requests\".\n- Your login lasts for that browser tab. Closing the tab logs you out.\n\n### 3.2 Who can do what\n\nThere are two roles.\n\n| Task | Editor | Owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| Add, edit and delete questions | Yes | Yes |\n| Create and edit quizzes (\"disciplines\"), show or hide them | Yes | Yes |\n| See the Hive tab and Stats | Yes | Yes |\n| Delete a whole quiz | No | Yes |\n| Hide a bee from the Hive TV | No | Yes |\n| Add and remove admins | No | Yes |\n| Delete scores older than 12 months | No | Yes |\n\n### 3.3 Adding and removing admins (owners only)\n\n1. Open the **Admins** tab.\n2. Under **Add someone**, enter the email, choose **Editor** or **Owner**, and select **Add**. They can log in straight away.\n3. To change someone's role, add their email again with the new role.\n4. To remove someone, select **Remove** next to their email, then select it again to confirm.\n\nYou can't remove or demote yourself. The database also prevents removal or demotion of the final owner, including concurrent requests. On 10 October 2026, the owner confirmed that `grindheadgames@gmail.com` should remain the owner; no additional address was supplied.\n\n### 3.4 Editing the arts quizzes\n\nThe arts quizzes are Theatre, Music, Dance and Visual Art. They remain editable in admin and available through the API. The learner website opens the transferable skills quiz directly. An arts round requested through the API contains up to 10 questions picked at random.\n\n1. Open the **Quizzes** tab and pick the quiz from the list on the left. Its details appear first.\n2. Select **Edit questions**, then select a question to open it. To add one, select **+ Add question**.\n3. Fill in:\n   - **Question**: up to 300 characters.\n   - **Answers**: 2 to 4. Leave a box empty to use fewer than 4.\n   - **The correct answer**: tick the circle next to it.\n   - **Fact shown after answering**: optional, up to 300 characters. Learners see it after each answer.\n   - **Review status**: Draft or Approved (see 3.6).\n4. Select **Save**. An \"Unsaved\" label shows until you do.\n\nTo delete a question, open it, select **Delete**, then select it again to confirm.\n\nSelect **Close questions** to return to the quiz details. Save any edits first, or confirm that you want to discard them. Selecting another quiz also closes the question list.\n\nEach arts quiz should have at least 10 questions. The list on the left shows how many each one has. An active quiz with fewer than 10 questions remains available through the API, but its rounds will be shorter.\n\n**Quiz settings.** Select **Settings** beside the quiz name to change the quiz name, blurb, list order and **Show to learners**. Untick **Show to learners** to disable public API access to a quiz without deleting it. Disabling **Discover Your Transferable Skills** prevents learners from starting the website's quiz. The short code can't be changed after the quiz is created. Changes to the public API quiz list can take up to a minute to appear.\n\n### 3.5 Editing the career quiz (Discover Your Transferable Skills)\n\nThis quiz has no right answers. Learners answer every question, in the order shown in admin. Each answer carries hidden \"tags\" that the server uses to work out the learner's result. Learners never see the tags.\n\n**How the result is worked out.** For each answer the learner picks:\n\n- its **main pathway** gets 2 points;\n- each of its **other pathways** gets 1 point;\n- each of its **roles** gets 1 point, counted under the main pathway;\n- each of its **strengths** gets 1 point.\n\nThe result shows the pathway with the most points and 2 to 4 roles to explore in it, a second pathway, and the learner's top 3 strengths. Ties go to the pathway higher up this list: Film & TV Production, Events & Live, Marketing Content & Communications, Design, Music, Theatre & Performance, Digital & Creative Technology. Learners can save or print their result, replay the same quiz, or watch the Hive TV.\n\nTo edit a question:\n\n1. Open the **Quizzes** tab and pick **Discover Your Transferable Skills**.\n2. Select **Edit questions**, then select a question to open it.\n3. Edit:\n   - **Scenario**: the first line, which sets the scene.\n   - **Question**: the question itself.\n   - **Answers A to E**: 2 to 5 answers. Clear an answer's text to remove it.\n   - For each answer:\n     - **Main pathway (2 points)**: required.\n     - **Also scores (1 point each)**: tick any other pathways.\n     - **Roles**: separated by commas, for example `Producer, Production Manager`.\n     - **Strengths**: separated by commas. The box suggests strengths already used elsewhere, so spellings stay consistent. Up to 8 roles and 8 strengths per answer.\n   - **Review status**.\n4. Select **Save**.\n\n**Reorder** questions with the **\u2191** and **\u2193** arrows next to each one. Save or discard any open changes first.\n\nThe 7 pathways themselves and their descriptions are set in the code (`backend/hive/career.js`), not in admin. Ask a developer to change them. The API retains arts quiz pairings, but the learner results screen does not display them.\n\n### 3.6 Review status\n\nAll questions are **Approved** (live), and new questions start as Approved. You can still mark a question **Draft** as a reminder for your team while you rework it, but this is a checklist only: **learners see every question in a shown quiz, whatever its status.** Select **Edit questions** to use the review filters and search box. To keep a quiz away from learners until it is ready, untick **Show to learners** in its settings.\n\n### 3.7 Hiding a bee from the Hive TV (owners only)\n\n1. Open the **Hive** tab. It shows the newest 300 bees.\n2. Find the bee. You can filter by Learners, AI or Hidden.\n3. Select **Hide**. The bee leaves the Hive TV within a few seconds.\n4. To bring it back, filter by **Hidden** and select **Show**.\n\nLearners can type bee names, which the server checks against a blocked-words list. Use **Hide** if a learner name or an AI bee's personality line still needs removing from the TV.\n\n### 3.8 Reading the stats\n\nThe **Stats** tab covers the last 12 months:\n\n- **Rounds played**: how many quizzes were finished.\n- **Learner bees in the Hive**: real (not AI) bees that aren't hidden.\n- **Average score by discipline**: plays and average score for each arts quiz. The career quiz shows \"n/a\" because it has no score.\n- **Career quiz: top pathways**: how many learners got each pathway as their strongest result. These counts start from 7 October 2026, when this was added.\n\nAll stats are anonymous. They are not linked to any learner. Moodle keeps its own per-learner records (see section 4).\n\n### 3.8a Insights\n\nThe **Insights** tab shows real data only (nothing is made up or seeded), for the last 7, 30, 90 or 365 days:\n\n- **Tiles:** quizzes completed (career and arts) and new learner bees.\n- **Activity by day:** quizzes completed each day (bars) and new bees (dots).\n- **Career pathway spread:** each learner's strongest pathway.\n- **Average score by arts quiz.**\n- **Most-missed arts questions:** questions answered at least 3 times, ranked by the share of wrong answers, with the correct answer shown. A very high rate can mean a hard question, or a wrong or unclear answer, so it's worth a check. (This counts from 8 October 2026, when it was added.)\n- **Download CSV:** every finished quiz in the period (date, quiz, score, total, percent, top pathway). No names, emails or ids are included.\n\nWhile there's little data, each panel says so. Insights need an admin login and are limited to 30 refreshes a minute per admin.\n\n### 3.9 Deleting old data (owners only)\n\nLearners are told that scores are kept for 12 months. To keep that promise:\n\n1. Open the **Stats** tab.\n2. Under **Data retention**, select **Delete scores older than 12 months**, then select it again to confirm.\n\nA Supabase Cron job now runs daily at 03:15 UTC and deletes anonymous plays older than 365 days. The owner approved this schedule on 10 October 2026. The manual button remains available. The job preserves bees, questions and admins, and also removes expired rate-limit bookkeeping and its own job logs older than 30 days. Check the job in Supabase Cron if retention monitoring reports a failure.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Putting it in Moodle or Totara\n\n**Step-by-step guide, with recommended settings and troubleshooting: [docs/LMS-SETUP.md](LMS-SETUP.md).** This section is the summary.\n\nThere are two ways. The SCORM package is recommended because Moodle can then record completion and scores.\n\n### 4.1 Build the SCORM package (developer, about a minute)\n\nOn a computer with the project and Node.js 20 or newer:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build:scorm\n```\n\nThis writes `dist/the-hive-scorm12.zip` (SCORM 1.2). By default it points at https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app, and any finished quiz marks the activity **completed**.\n\nOptions:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build:scorm -- --pass 70                          # passed/failed at 70% on the arts quizzes\nnpm run build:scorm -- https://hive.example.org --pass 70  # a different web address\nnpm run build:scorm -- --title \"The Hive\"                  # the title Moodle shows\n```\n\nYou only need to rebuild if the web address, pass mark or title changes. Editing questions never needs a new package. The deploy script (section 7.5) also rebuilds it.\n\n### 4.2 Upload it to Moodle\n\nExact names vary a little between Moodle and Totara versions.\n\n1. In the course, turn editing on.\n2. Select **Add an activity or resource**, then **SCORM package**.\n3. Give it a name, and upload `the-hive-scorm12.zip` under **Package file**.\n4. **Appearance**:\n   - **Display package**: **Current window** works best. The game needs at least 640 pixels of height.\n   - Hide the course structure and navigation, as there is only one page.\n   - If you choose **New window**, make it at least 1000 \u00d7 750 pixels.\n5. **Attempts management**:\n   - **Number of attempts**: Unlimited.\n   - **Grading method**: Highest grade.\n   - **Force new attempt**: No. The package keeps the learner's best score in an attempt anyway.\n6. **Activity completion**: choose **Students must receive a status** and tick **Completed**. Learners currently play the career quiz, which always reports *Completed* (it has no score), so **don't require Passed**: nobody would ever complete the activity. (`--pass` only matters if the scored arts quizzes are offered to learners again.) Also set the SCORM **Height** to at least 700 pixels if you embed it in the current window, because the launcher needs about 670.\n7. Save, then try it as a student (or with \"Log in as\") before telling learners.\n\n### 4.3 What Moodle records\n\nFor an **arts quiz**:\n\n- **Score**: the percentage correct, 0 to 100. If the learner plays more than once, the best score is kept.\n- **Status**: **completed** after any finished quiz. With `--pass`, it is **passed** or **failed** instead.\n- **Interactions** (Moodle: the activity's **Reports**, then **Interactions**): one line per question, with the learner's answer, the correct answer and right or wrong.\n\nFor the **career quiz**:\n\n- **Status**: **completed**, with no score. A \"passed\" already recorded is kept.\n- **Interactions**: one line per question with the letter chosen (a to e), marked \"neutral\".\n- **Comments**: the learner's latest result, in the form \"Creative Career Discovery: *pathway* (*suggested role*). Strengths: *top 3 strengths*\".\n\nMoodle also records time spent. It does not send learner names or other Moodle details to the Hive. Learners can type their own bee names, so the game asks them to use made-up names.\n\nThe launcher has an **Open The Hive in a new window** link. Anything played in that separate window is **not** recorded in Moodle.\n\n### 4.4 The plain alternative: a link or an iframe\n\nYou can also link to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app with a Moodle **URL** resource, or embed it in a **Page** with an iframe, for example:\n\n```html\n<iframe src=\"https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/\" title=\"The Hive\" style=\"width:100%;height:800px;border:0\" allow=\"fullscreen\"></iframe>\n```\n\nThis is simpler, but **Moodle records nothing**: no scores, no completion beyond \"viewed\".\n\n### 4.5 Framing rules\n\n- The game is allowed to be shown inside other sites. The site's security settings say `frame-ancestors *`, which means any site may frame it. That is what lets Moodle and the SCORM launcher show it.\n- To allow only your Moodle, a developer can change `frame-ancestors *` in `vercel.json` to your Moodle address (for example `frame-ancestors https://learning.example.org`) and redeploy.\n- The admin pages can never be framed (`frame-ancestors 'none'` and `X-Frame-Options: DENY`). Always open admin in its own tab.\n- Inside Moodle, some browsers keep the game's storage separate from a direct visit. So a learner's bee may not be remembered between Moodle and a direct visit. Their bee still joins the Hive.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Running the Hive TV in the building\n\n### Set-up\n\n1. On the laptop connected to the TV, open **https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/tv** in Chrome or Edge.\n2. Go full screen. Press **F11** (or use your browser's full-screen option), or select the **Full screen** button in the corner. F11 is better for a screen left running, because it stays on after the page reloads itself (see below). The on-screen button's full screen ends when the page reloads.\n3. Turn off sleep and screen-saver in the laptop's power settings. The page also asks the browser to keep the screen awake, but not all browsers do this.\n\nThe mouse pointer and the corner buttons hide after 3 seconds without movement. Move the mouse to bring them back.\n\n### What's on screen\n\n- Every visible bee flying. New learner bees get a \"Just landed\" spotlight. In a busy Hive only the newest 24 learner bees are named.\n- A count of real bees (\"bees in the Hive\").\n- A scrolling strip with the web address, the latest bee names and the quiz names.\n- A **QR code** (\"Join the Hive\"). Scanning it opens the game on a phone. It points at the address the TV page was opened from, so open the TV from the address you want people to use.\n\n### Pause motion\n\nThe **Pause motion** button stops the swarm, the scrolling strip and the blinking. Select **Play motion** to start them again. This is an accessibility feature for people affected by movement. The setting is remembered on that laptop's browser. If the laptop's system is set to \"reduce motion\", the TV starts paused.\n\n### Daily reload\n\nThe page reloads itself once every 24 hours after it was opened. This picks up any new version of the site without anyone touching the laptop.\n\n### If the network drops\n\nThe bees already on screen keep flying. After two failed checks, \"Reconnecting\u2026\" appears at the bottom. The page keeps retrying, at most every 30 seconds, and carries on by itself when the connection returns. If the page has gone completely blank, reload it (F5).\n\nIf the database (Supabase) is down, the TV behaves the same way: it keeps the bees it has and shows \"Reconnecting\u2026\".\n\n---\n\n## 6. The real logo and the Gr\u00e4benbach fonts\n\n### 6.1 The Factory International logo\n\nThe **Third Party Use** version of the Factory International logo is live (8 October 2026). It was taken unaltered, as vector artwork, from the brand guidelines PDF (*01 Guidelines_3rd party*, page 8). It was not redrawn.\n\n- `public/img/fi-logo-third-party-black.svg`: black, for light pages. It sits in the game's footer, beside the small print.\n- `public/img/fi-logo-third-party-white.svg`: white, for the dark Hive TV. It sits bottom left, above the ticker.\n- `public/img/logo.json` switches them on: `\"src\"` is used by the game and `\"srcOnDark\"` by the Hive TV. To turn the logo off, set both to `null` and deploy.\n\nThe guidelines' rules are built in:\n\n- **Clear space:** one fifth of the logo's width on every side. This is part of each SVG file's own frame, so no layout can crowd it.\n- **Minimum size:** 70 pixels wide for this version. The files are always shown at least 98 pixels wide, so the logo itself never goes below 70 pixels. It is about 86 pixels wide on the game on a desktop, 74 on a phone, and 70 to 93 on the Hive TV.\n- **Never over busy graphics:** it sits on plain white (game) or plain black (TV).\n\nIf Factory International sends updated files, replace the two SVGs and keep the same names, or update `logo.json`. If you change a file's name, use a new name rather than reusing an old one: Vercel's cache can briefly keep serving an old \"not found\" for a previously requested address.\n\n### 6.2 The Gr\u00e4benbach fonts\n\nFactory International's brand typefaces are Gr\u00e4benbach and Gr\u00e4benbach Mono (from Camelot Typefaces). **The web licence is confirmed (8 October 2026)**, so the fonts are on across the site. Arial, the guidelines' approved system font, is the fallback while the fonts load.\n\nHow the switch works today:\n\n- The font files are already in the project (`public/fonts/`, WOFF2 and WOFF, Latin subset).\n- The game and the TV use Gr\u00e4benbach **unless** the setting `BRAND_FONTS` is exactly `off`. If `BRAND_FONTS` is missing or has any other value, the fonts are on.\n- Technically, the pages start with the class `brand-fonts` on the `<html>` element, then remove it if the server says `BRAND_FONTS=off`.\n- The admin pages always use Gr\u00e4benbach, whatever the setting.\n\nTo switch them off for any reason, set `BRAND_FONTS` to `off` for Production in Vercel (or in `.env`, then run the deploy script) and redeploy. The font files stay published either way.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Technical reference (technical)\n\n### 7.1 What's in the project\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `backend/puzzle-punks/` | Separate Puzzle Punks Elixir repository, preserved during consolidation and excluded from the Hive deployment |\n| `public/` | The static site: `index.html` (game), `tv.html`, `admin.html`, `css/`, `js/`, `fonts/`, `img/` |\n| `api/index.js` | The single Vercel function. It re-exports `backend/hive/handler.js`. |\n| `backend/hive/handler.js` | Every API route, validation, rate limits, admin auth and AI bees |\n| `backend/hive/store.js` | Data layer: Supabase store, in-memory store, and the seed fallback |\n| `backend/hive/career.js` | Career pathways and scoring |\n| `backend/hive/seed-data.js` | Quiz content: the 4 arts quizzes and the career quiz |\n| `backend/hive/career-discovery-questions.json` | The client's career quiz (15 questions, 5 tagged answers each) |\n| `backend/hive/local.js` | Local dev server (`npm run dev`) |\n| `supabase/*.sql` | Schema, migrations and content seeds |\n| `scorm/` | SCORM 1.2 launcher templates |\n| `scripts/` | Deploy, SCORM build and content scripts |\n| `tests/` | Node test runner tests |\n\nThe server uses the official Anthropic SDK for optional AI features. It is plain Node 20+ with static HTML, CSS and JavaScript, and no build step for the site.\n\n### 7.2 Running locally\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev     # http://localhost:3000  (game /, Hive TV /tv, admin /admin)\n```\n\n- `npm run dev` explicitly opts into **in-memory** local storage when neither Supabase setting is present. Hosted deployments or partial credentials fail closed with 503. It is seeded with the bundled content and resets when you stop it.\n- In memory mode, admin login sends no email. Entering `grindheadgames@gmail.com` (the built-in owner) logs you straight in with a local dev token.\n- If a `.env` file exists with `SUPABASE_URL` and `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY`, the local server uses that Supabase project, which may be the live data. Be careful.\n- `PORT` and `HOST` change the port and address (default `127.0.0.1:3000`).\n\n### 7.3 Tests\n\n```bash\nnpm test\n```\n\nThis runs the Node test runner over `tests/*.test.js`, using the in-memory store. The tests cover:\n\n- the API, bees and admin roles (`api.test.js`);\n- question editing for both quiz types (`admin-edit.test.js`);\n- career content and scoring, stats and the logo switch (`career.test.js`);\n- the SCORM build (`scorm.test.js`);\n- same-site writes, headers, rate limits, the Supabase-down fallback and the CSP framing rules (`security.test.js`).\n\n### 7.4 Environment variables\n\nSet these in Vercel (**Project \u2192 Settings \u2192 Environment Variables**, Production) and, for local use, in `.env`. `.env.example` lists them. Never commit `.env`. Vercel only picks up changes on the next deploy.\n\n| Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `SUPABASE_URL` | The Supabase project URL, `https://<ref>.supabase.co`. With `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY`, it switches the app from memory to Supabase. |\n| `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY` | The Supabase secret key (`sb_secret_\u2026`, or the legacy `service_role` key). **Server only.** It bypasses all database security. |\n| `SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` | The publishable (or legacy `anon`) key. Used only by the server, for the admin login emails and token checks. |\n| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | Claude API key for host commentary, career coaching, admin drafts and AI bees. |\n| `ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID` | Workspace ID required by organization-scoped Anthropic keys. |\n| `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` | Optional. Overrides the Claude model used by the AI agents and AI bees (the default is set in `backend/hive/agents.js`). |\n| `PUBLIC_URL` | Optional. The site's public address, used for the admin login link and by the deploy script. Set it when you move to a custom domain. |\n| `BRAND_FONTS` | `off` switches the Gr\u00e4benbach fonts off on the game and TV. Any other value, or none, leaves them on (section 6.2). |\n| `BLOCKED_WORDS` | Optional. Extra comma-separated words that learner bee names, AI bee names and AI personalities must not contain. A built-in list always applies. |\n\nUsed only by scripts on your own computer, never sent to Vercel:\n\n| Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` | A Supabase personal access token (`sbp_\u2026`). `scripts/deploy-now.sh` uses it to read and update the Auth URLs, preserving existing redirect entries. |\n| `PORT`, `HOST` | Local dev server only. |\n\n### 7.5 Deploying\n\nThe site deploys to the Vercel project **buzz-tv**, which serves https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app. You need the Vercel CLI login (`npx vercel login`) and a `.env` file.\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/deploy-now.sh\n```\n\nWhat it does, in order:\n\n1. Loads `.env` and requires all three Supabase settings: URL, publishable key and secret key. Supply these before running; the helper does not fetch or save credentials.\n2. Checks that `.vercel/project.json` identifies **buzz-tv** in the expected Vercel team, then inspects that linked project.\n3. Replaces the Production Supabase settings and `PUBLIC_URL` with the supplied values. The public URL must use HTTPS and defaults to https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app. `BRAND_FONTS` defaults to `on`.\n4. Updates optional AI, model, blocked-word and Google Sheets settings only when they are present in `.env`. Empty or absent optional values leave existing Vercel settings in place. To disable AI explicitly, remove the Production key in Vercel.\n5. If `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` is supplied, updates Supabase Auth's Site URL and adds `/admin` and `/admin/**` to its redirects, preserving other entries. Without it, configure the Auth URLs separately.\n6. Builds the SCORM package for the chosen public address, then deploys to the fixed **buzz-tv** project in the correct team.\n7. Checks the canonical health endpoint and fails unless Supabase storage reports healthy. Each failed command stops the helper. Output goes to the terminal; redirect it yourself if you need a log.\n\nAfter deploying, check that https://buzz-tv-six.vercel.app/api/health shows `\"storage\":\"supabase\"`. `\"ai\":true` means an Anthropic key is set.\n\n`scripts/deploy-wizard.sh` now uses the existing Buzz TV project and correct Vercel scope, runs the complete new-database migration order, defaults licensed fonts to on, preserves absent optional settings, and includes Sheets settings and both admin redirect patterns. `deploy-now.sh` uses the same fixed project, validates its link, fails on configuration errors, and preserves absent optional variables.\n\n### 7.6 Database set-up and migrations\n\nFor a new Supabase project:\n\n1. In the Supabase **SQL editor**, run the files in `supabase/` **in this order**:\n   1. `01_schema.sql`: tables, row-level security, and the first owner (`grindheadgames@gmail.com`).\n   2. `03_career_quiz.sql`: adds profile quizzes (`disciplines.mode`, `questions.scoring`, up to 5 answers).\n   3. `02_seed.sql`: the four arts quizzes (20 questions each). It only inserts into quizzes that have no questions yet.\n   4. `04_career_seed.sql`: the career quiz. **Re-running it deletes and replaces all career quiz questions**, including any edits made in admin.\n   5. `05_play_pathway.sql`: adds `plays.pathway` for the career stats.\n   6. `06_publish_all.sql`: marks every question approved (live) and makes that the default.\n   7. `07_play_answers.sql`: adds `plays.asked` and `plays.missed` (question ids only) for the most-missed questions in Insights.\n\n   8. `08_operational_safeguards.sql`: server-only shared rate limits and database protection for the final owner.\n   9. `09_retention_schedule.sql`: the approved daily 365-day purge and expired-limit cleanup.\n   10. `10_content_corrections.sql`: the six approved arts content corrections, matching only original fields.\n   11. `11_retry_safe_bees.sql`: atomically saves a bee and play with a durable hashed retry receipt.\n\n   `01_schema.sql` already includes the columns that `03`, `05` and `07` add, so on a new project those two make no changes. They are safe to re-run, and they are what upgrades an older database.\n2. In **Authentication \u2192 URL Configuration**, set **Site URL** to the live address and add `https://<address>/admin` to **Redirect URLs**. `deploy-now.sh` can do this for you.\n3. In **Project Settings \u2192 API Keys**, copy the project URL, the publishable key and a secret key into `.env` and Vercel.\n4. Deploy, then check `/api/health`.\n\nThe tables are `disciplines`, `questions`, `bees`, `plays` and `admins`. Admin logins also create users in Supabase Auth. Removing someone from the admin list blocks them, but it leaves their Auth user in place. Delete that in **Authentication \u2192 Users** if you want it gone.\n\n### 7.7 Content seed scripts\n\nThe quiz content in the code (`backend/hive/seed-data.js` and `backend/hive/career-discovery-questions.json`) is used three ways:\n\n- as the in-memory data for local dev and tests;\n- as the fallback content if Supabase is down (section 7.9);\n- as the source of the SQL seeds.\n\nOnce the site is live, **admin is the place to edit questions**. The scripts below are for set-up and for re-loading the client's career content.\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `npm run seed:sql` | Regenerates `supabase/02_seed.sql` and `supabase/04_career_seed.sql` from the code. |\n| `node scripts/push-career.js` | Pushes the career quiz from the code straight into the Supabase project in `.env`. It adds the new questions first, then removes the old ones, so the quiz is never left empty. **This overwrites any admin edits to the career quiz.** It needs `03_career_quiz.sql` applied first. |\n\n### 7.8 Security notes\n\n- **Database locked down.** Row-level security is on for all application tables, with no policies, and all rights are taken away from the `anon` and `authenticated` roles. A public key can read or write nothing. Only the server, with the secret key, can.\n- **The secret key is server-only.** It lives in Vercel's environment and `.env`. It never appears in the browser or the code. The browser only ever calls the site's own `/api/...`.\n- **Same-site JSON writes.** Every write must be sent as JSON (otherwise \"415\"). If a browser on another site tries it, it is refused (\"403\"). So other web pages can't post bees or plays. The API doesn't grant cross-site access (no CORS headers).\n- **Admin access.** Login uses Supabase Auth email one-time links. On every admin request the server checks the token with Supabase, then checks the email against the `admins` table and its role. The login reply is the same whether or not the email is on the list.\n- **Rate limits** (per network address):\n\n  | What | Limit |\n  |---|---|\n  | Admin login emails | 5 per 10 minutes |\n  | Saving a bee (new or updated) | 120 per minute (generous, as a whole office may share one address) |\n  | New bees | 60 per 10 minutes |\n  | Career quiz scoring | 40 per minute |\n  | AI bee requests | 4 per minute, plus at least 45 seconds between batches, 3 bees per batch, and at most 30 AI bees |\n\n- **Headers** (`vercel.json`):\n  - A strict Content Security Policy: only the site's own scripts, styles, fonts and connections.\n  - `frame-ancestors *` for the game and TV, so Moodle can frame them.\n  - `frame-ancestors 'none'` plus `X-Frame-Options: DENY` for admin.\n  - Also `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, a strict `Referrer-Policy`, a `Permissions-Policy` that blocks camera, microphone, location and payment, and `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy`.\n  - API responses also send `Cache-Control: no-store` (except the public lists below), `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`.\n- **Learner input is limited.**\n  - Bee names must have 2 to 24 Unicode characters, start with a letter, and use letters, digits, spaces, apostrophes or hyphens. `POST /api/bee-name` validates and moderates them before the quiz starts. Publication checks the name again.\n  - Bee designs must use the known options.\n  - Scores are range-checked.\n  - The career quiz tags never reach the browser, and the result is worked out on the server.\n  - AI bees are checked against the same design options, a name pattern and a blocked-words list.\n- **Learner privacy.** Plays are anonymous, and Moodle sends no learner details to the Hive. Bee names are public on the TV, so the game asks learners to invent names rather than use real names. User-entered names may still contain personal information. A random \"edit key\" on the learner's device lets them update their own bee. Only a hash of it is stored. Admin emails are stored in the `admins` table and Supabase Auth.\n- **Edge caching.** Vercel caches the quiz list and site config for up to 60 seconds, and the Hive for 2 seconds. This keeps the database quiet when many people play at once. Rounds, scoring and admin are never cached.\n\n### 7.9 If Supabase is down\n\n- **Learners can still read the quiz.** The quiz list and questions come from the content bundled in the code: the arts quizzes and the career quiz as in `backend/hive/seed-data.js`. Admin edits are **not** included. The score and publication endpoints require the shared limiter to be available, so the learner may need to retry when Supabase returns.\n- **Bees can't be saved.** At the end, the learner sees \"your bee couldn't reach the Hive\" with a **Try again** button. Their score is still shown, and still sent to Moodle.\n- **No stats are recorded** for those plays.\n- **The Hive TV** keeps the bees it has and shows \"Reconnecting\u2026\".\n- **Admin is unavailable** (no login, no editing).\n- `/api/health` returns an error, so it is a quick way to check.\n\nThe fallback only applies to outages: network errors, Supabase server errors (5xx) and Supabase rate limiting (429). Other database errors still show as errors. Everything recovers by itself when Supabase returns.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Known issues and next steps\n\n### Content\n\n- **All content is live (8 October 2026).** Every question is approved and no \"draft\" labels are shown to learners.\n- **Arts content corrections completed, 10 October 2026.** The owner approved all six factual corrections in [docs/CONTENT-QA.md](CONTENT-QA.md). They are applied to the code, seed SQL and live questions.\n- **Authored career copy approved.** The owner confirmed client approval of the seven pathway descriptions on 13 October 2026. V1 scoring tags and Q15E retain their recorded 12 October approval. Final-location deployment is deferred, and physical Safari testing is waived. Parts of the career quiz were written by us rather than supplied: the **strengths** tags, the **pathway descriptions** (`backend/hive/career.js`) and **Q15 answer E** (the client's document was cut off). Edit them if the client wants different wording. The API also retains arts quiz pairings written by us; these are not shown on the learner website.\n- The game's loading message names `buzz-tv-six.vercel.app`. Update it in `public/index.html` if the site moves to a new address.\n\n### Brand\n\n- **The supplied Factory International logo is live.** See section 6.1.\n- **Gr\u00e4benbach fonts:** licence confirmed and the fonts are live. See section 6.2.\n\n### Decisions log\n\n- **8 October 2026: bees grow from answers.** The costume builder and unlockable skins were removed. Bees are generated from quiz answers (section 1.1). The `skin` column on quizzes is no longer used.\n- **8 October 2026: automatic bee updates.** Removed the results-screen name selector. The live career bee and final result use the same pathway weights, tie rules and strengths, including when a learner changes an earlier answer.\n- **8 October 2026: welcome flow and custom names.** The home page shows **Build Your Bee**, **Discover Your Transferable Skills**, and **Join The Hive**, with an editable made-up name and a **Next** button. Names are validated and moderated before the quiz starts and before publication. Same-device replay can rename and regrow the existing bee, and the Hive TV updates its name and design.\n- **8 October 2026: direct learner quiz.** **Next** opens the first transferable skills question. The category chooser, category replay buttons and arts quiz suggestions were removed from the learner website. Admin editing and arts quiz APIs remain available.\n- **8 October 2026: content live.** All questions approved; no \"draft\" labels; Gr\u00e4benbach licence confirmed.\n\n### Technical\n\n- **AI enabled on 13 October 2026.** Production uses the approved key and workspace with `claude-opus-5-5`; `/api/health` reports `\"ai\":true`. Deployed host commentary and career coaching returned AI responses. Admin drafting and background-bee creation passed against the real provider in disposable memory. Live background-bee creation was not tested because automatic approval review rejected adding production records. The key expires on 12 November 2026 at 14:41 GMT. See `docs/evidence/2026-10-13-vercel-release/summary.json`.\n- **Rate limits are shared across production servers.** An atomic Supabase RPC stores hashed keys with short expiry. RLS and grants restrict it to the server. Expired bookkeeping is removed in bounded batches on requests and by the daily job. In-memory development retains local limits. An unavailable shared limiter does not bypass the cap.\n- **Career stats only count from 7 October 2026**, when the top pathway started being recorded. Career quizzes finished before then count as plays but have no pathway.\n- **The 365-day purge runs daily at 03:15 UTC**, with a manual admin control retained (section 3.9).\n- **Owner confirmed, 10 October 2026.** Keep `grindheadgames@gmail.com` as the owner, as requested.\n- **Owner protection is enforced by the API and database.** The API refuses self-removal and self-demotion. Database statement triggers serialize admin changes and refuse loss of the final owner, including bulk writes and truncation.\n- **Deployment scripts updated, 10 October 2026** (section 7.5).\n- **Live admin security headers verified, 10 October 2026.** The production page sends `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and a CSP with `frame-ancestors 'none'`.\n- **An active arts quiz with fewer than 10 questions remains available through the API**, with shorter rounds (section 3.4).\n- **Review status doesn't hide questions** from learners (section 3.6).\n\n---\n\n## 9. The AI agents (Claude)\n\nThe Hive has four optional AI features, written by Claude (Anthropic) through the official Anthropic SDK. They are enabled in production as of 13 October 2026. If the provider is unavailable, the learner experience uses hand-written fallback text.\n\n| Agent | Where | What it does | Without a key |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Host bee** | Hive TV | Greets each new bee in the \"Just landed\" banner, comments on what's happening (new arrivals, the most popular pathway today, today's top arts score) and shows a one-line \"state of the Hive\" under the title. | Hand-written greetings, comments and summary. |\n| **Career coach** | Career results | One personal paragraph and 2\u20133 next steps, based on the learner's strongest pathway, roles and strengths. Labelled \"Written for you by Claude, an AI coach\". | The pathway description and three hand-written next steps per pathway. |\n| **Draft with AI** | Admin > a quiz > Edit questions | Drafts one question (and, for the career quiz, its scoring tags) into a new, **unsaved** form. Nothing goes live until an admin checks it and selects Save. | The button explains that AI drafting is switched off. |\n| **AI bees** | Hive TV | Invents a few bees while the Hive has fewer than 30 real ones. | No AI bees. |\n\n### 9.1 Switching them on\n\n1. Create an API key in the Claude Console (platform.claude.com).\n2. In Vercel: **Project > Settings > Environment Variables**, add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for Production (or add it to `.env` and run `scripts/deploy-now.sh`, which copies it to Vercel).\n3. Redeploy. To switch the agents off again, remove the variable and redeploy.\n\n`ANTHROPIC_MODEL` is optional: it overrides the default model set in `backend/hive/agents.js`.\n\n### 9.2 How they work (technical)\n\nAll the code and the exact prompts are in `backend/hive/agents.js` (AI bees: `askClaudeForBees` in `backend/hive/handler.js`). Every call:\n\n- runs **on the server only**: the browser never sees the key or talks to Anthropic;\n- uses low effort and the server-side **refusal fallback** (if Claude declines, another model tries; if that also declines, or the call fails or times out, the hand-written text is used);\n- checks the reply: lines must be short, family-friendly (the same word filter as bee names) and in the expected shape, or the hand-written text is used.\n\nPrompts, in short:\n\n- **Host bee:** \"You are the host bee on The Hive TV\u2026 warm, witty, family-friendly lines in UK English\u2026 never mention people, only bees by their bee names\u2026 don't invent facts beyond the snapshot.\" It sees only bee names, what they're wearing, counts, today's top pathway and top arts score.\n- **Career coach:** \"You are a warm, practical careers coach for the learning hub of Factory International\u2026 use only the profile you are given\u2026 do not invent specific jobs, vacancies, courses, programmes, events, people or contacts at Factory International or anywhere else\u2026 suggest general, realistic next steps.\" It sees only the quiz outcome: pathway names and descriptions, roles and strengths. **No personal data** (no name, bee name, email, IP address or Moodle details) is sent, and the server re-scores the answers itself, so only a genuine quiz result is used.\n- **Draft with AI:** \"You help staff at Factory International write questions\u2026 factually safe\u2026 must not make claims about Factory International itself.\" It sees the quiz name, the existing questions (to avoid repeats) and an optional topic. Drafts go through the same checks as hand-written questions and are marked Draft.\n\n### 9.3 Cost controls\n\n| Agent | Limits |\n|---|---|\n| Host bee | Refreshes at most every 3 minutes, and only when the Hive has changed (or every 15 minutes). At most 12 calls an hour per server. Replies are cached at Vercel's edge for 30 seconds, so many TVs share them. 20-second timeout. |\n| Career coach | One call per finished career quiz. 12 per network and 4 per browser tab every 10 minutes. 12-second timeout (the learner sees a loading state, then the hand-written steps if it's slow). |\n| Draft with AI | Admins only. 20 drafts an hour per admin. 30-second timeout. |\n| AI bees | Up to 3 bees per call, at most every 45 seconds, only while a Hive TV is open and there are fewer than 30 real bees. |\n\nAll calls use low effort. Coach, admin draft and AI seed request limits are shared in Supabase. Host cache and host refresh limits remain per instance.\n\n"
